INSERT INTO articles (id, subcategory_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (113, 113, 'People''s perceptions of why the suicide took place', '<h1>Bereavement due to suicide</h1>
      <h2>People''s perceptions of why the suicide took place</h2><div class=''content''><p>After a suicide, friends and relatives want to know why it occurred and try to make sense of what happened.</p>

<p>Many people told us that they thought their family member had ended their life because they had been feeling unhappy or depressed.</p>

<p>Bereaved relatives suggested many reasons that might have contributed to such feelings, including bullying at school, loss of a job, stress at work, a move to a new town, a lack of support, unhappy or violent relationships, financial difficulties, negative reactions to family events, and chronic illness.</p>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tText v4x3 hideit''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653285');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2209, 113, 'Colin and Barbara''s son, Matt, was not happy as a junior doctor. He found it hard to cope.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''textOnly''><div>Text only<div><img src=''/img/book.png''/></div><span>Read below</span></div></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2210, 113, 'Colin and Barbara''s son, Matt, was not happy as a junior doctor. He found it hard to cope.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>HIDE TEXT</div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>I have the feeling that I bear some responsibility for the sad event with which we have lived ever since, in that I suspect because I had seen how much his big sister had enjoyed doing medicine I rather pressured Matt to follow her footsteps, and he was polymathic, he could&rsquo;ve done anything from astrophysics to Chinese as it were, but he could equally do medicine and did. So it seemed, but it became clear as he went through medicine that even though he graduated perfectly easily and went into his first house job that he was uneasy with it, and despite his great abilities and praise heaped on him by his own consultant he was not comfortable with it and gave up within the first year. I think he had an unfortunate experience, junior house officers were particularly exploited at that time, things have got easier since, but his partner&rsquo;s father died and she was off like a flash to look after that area of need in her family, so that he was left very exposed in the job that he was doing and going back to a house with no company at the end of a long day, all of these pressures got to him and he came home to us,&nbsp;confused, unhappy, and hoping for some recognition from the&nbsp; university clinical department which had placed him in his house job, for some support and recognition. That did not come, and I think that contributed to the depression which afflicted him thereafter.</p>

<p><strong>Why recognition, what, what was he hoping?</strong></p>

<p>He was hoping that the University Clinical Department would&rsquo;ve placed him somewhere better supported in his house job, than the one in which he was suffering at the [provincial] hospital.</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2211, 113, 'Jane thinks that her son Tom had been bullied at school and was a bit of a depressive.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653285');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2212, 113, 'Jane thinks that her son Tom had been bullied at school and was a bit of a depressive.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>Right, it was nearly 23 years ago so some of it fades. He was at school at the time and we knew he was a bit of a depressive. He&rsquo;d chosen to paint his bedroom purple which people said was worrying, but that was his choice and there were tiny signs that we found out afterwards but not, anyway it was a time of upheaval for us because my husband had been made redundant and our son Tom was in his A&rsquo; level year so there were you know stresses, and then we were out on a Saturday night in London and he was at boarding, part time boarding, nearby and we got back and there was a phone call to phone the school. And my husband did it straight away, and I just heard him say, &ldquo;Suicide.&rdquo; And that was it.</p>

<p>I suppose I haven&rsquo;t revisited it because it didn&rsquo;t seem important, it&hellip; it seemed important dealing with the stuff around me and I did try and understand;&nbsp; Tom was bullied at school, one master gave him a hard time,&nbsp; so that, you asked earlier about anger, that would&rsquo;ve been relevant. I mean I remember saying to somebody that if I met the chap, he was only a small chap and I said I hope I don&rsquo;t meet him &lsquo;cos I&rsquo;d kill him.</p>

<p><strong>Mm.</strong></p>

<p>But you know I think it was only a contributory factor it wasn&rsquo;t a cause, it was just you know another brick in the wall, which is back to the, &hellip;what&rsquo;s that group I mentioned earlier? It was their theme song that we played.</p>

<p><br />
<strong>Oh yes.</strong></p>

<p><br />
That we played at the end of the funeral.</p>

<p><br />
<strong>Why was that&hellip;</strong></p>

<p><br />
&hellip;the funeral, the brick in the wall.</p>

<p><br />
<strong>Was that a song that he liked?</strong></p>

<p><br />
Mm.</p>

<p><br />
<strong>In particular?</strong></p>

<p><br />
Mm. So but it, it was relevant, I mean there were a lot of things that were difficult at the time, he wasn&rsquo;t a high flyer at school so doing A&rsquo; levels was some stress I suppose, and then father redundant and we were thinking that we might go abroad at the time to live so there were a lot of; and unfortunately he used to come back from school, he only boarded sometimes, at 9 o&rsquo;clock when always the news was on, and the news it was when the Biafran famine was on, it was just awful night after night, I&rsquo;m sure that must&rsquo;ve, with hindsight had an effect on him.</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2213, 113, 'Mike thinks his father was unhappy at the time of his suicide: He had had an unhappy second marriage and had been made redundant at work.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653177');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2214, 113, 'Mike thinks his father was unhappy at the time of his suicide: He had had an unhappy second marriage and had been made redundant at work.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>In the house that day was my sister, and my brother-in-law, and well we, we’d known that my father was unhappy, to say the least, he’d, he’d made a second marriage, my mother had died about three years previously, and he was very devoted to my mother and he made a second marriage, which unfortunately was a disaster. He said himself he’d, he’d, he’d married in haste and was now repenting in leisure and it was a big mistake, he was very unhappy in his marriage. And then the, the next big thing was he was made redundant at work, and he’d worked very hard, he’d, he’d started off right at the bottom in this firm, metal refining firm, in Wolverhampton and had worked his way up to being the Managing Director on the firm. And the firm got taken over and the next thing we know he was being made redundant, and within what, three days of that happening he’d taken his life [sighs]. So we knew he was very unhappy with his circumstances, with the second wife where, you know, the marriage had been a disaster really and then this issue of losing his job, I think that was the, the final thing really, you know?</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p><strong>Mmm.</strong></p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p>And it was a great surprise because he was a very strong person, a very tough capable man who’d had a lot of blows and difficulties in his life, and I think this was the thing that we found so hard to understand, I mean I know now that depression and mental distress can hit literally anyone and all it takes is enough pressure and enough problems and in it the toughest of people will break.</p>
</section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tAudio v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2215, 113, 'Jacqui''s husband worried about everything; he had been diagnosed with depression and anxiety. Jenny says he was a perfectionist and may not have been able to face the idea he might not get 100% better.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653593');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2216, 113, 'Jacqui''s husband worried about everything; he had been diagnosed with depression and anxiety. Jenny says he was a perfectionist and may not have been able to face the idea he might not get 100% better.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>Well he had previously attempted suicide twice before in 1994.</p>

<p><strong>Mmm.</strong></p>

<p>&hellip;and that was when he was first diagnosed with depression and anxiety, so really all that, all those years it&rsquo;s been a case of, watching him, making sure he&rsquo;s okay &lsquo;cause he was always, always anxious, always anxious and he worried about everything, not like the state of the world or anything like that but he worried about the kids and if something, if, you know, is, if, that, whatever little crisis there was, oh I shouldn&rsquo;t really say a little crisis &lsquo;cause it was always a big crisis to him, you know, then it would just move, he would just move on to something else to worry about.</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tAudio v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2217, 113, 'Marion''s husband had not told her about his financial worries or history of mental health problems.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653662');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2218, 113, 'Marion''s husband had not told her about his financial worries or history of mental health problems.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>I found out afterwards in the, in the May afterwards when I was speaking to his elder brother that he had had mental health problems in the past. But we were married nearly 25 years and I knew nothing. I knew nothing. It was all very, very strange, totally unreal, totally unreal. And I also discovered that there were horrendous financial problems as well about which I knew nothing.</p>

<p><strong>Do you think that had been worrying him?</strong></p>

<p>Oh I think so, yes, I think so, but yeah that&rsquo;s, that&rsquo;s what happened.</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tAudio v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2219, 113, 'Paula thinks her husband was depressed and that that he experienced paranoid delusions.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653556');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2220, 113, 'Paula thinks her husband was depressed and that that he experienced paranoid delusions.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>Anyway I mean, what went on I don&rsquo;t really know. I think I suspected he was depressed. I know I suspected he was depressed. Myself, do you know, during our various fights. But I think it came up probably fairly inevitably as a result of that kind of thing. I used to say, &ldquo;Look you&rsquo;ve got to get help for this. I&rsquo;m sure you&rsquo;re depressed. You&rsquo;ve got to get help.&rdquo; And this is about seven years ago. And I mean it&rsquo;s seven years ago that we moved out of London.</p>

<p>But there was one thing you see, he suffered from what I believe to be paranoid delusions. He was locking up the window shutters in the middle of the day to stop people looking in the window. And we live in a very lonely, quiet street and nobody looks in the window. And if they do it&rsquo;s a neighbour. But he, he had in his mind threatening kind of people looking in the window and he also had in his mind that if he went back to work in Europe again that the secret police would come from his country to take him back.</p>

<p><strong>Had he ever sought any outside help from anybody, medical?</strong></p>

<p>He wouldn&rsquo;t take an aspirin if he had a headache.</p>

<p><strong>So he&rsquo;d never been for any sort of psychiatric help or counselling.</strong></p>

<p>No.</p>

<p><strong>With his GP?</strong></p>

<p>No. Absolutely not. No, no, I mean I, he sprained an ankle once and went to a doctor for that when he was in Europe. And it also, it turned out. I mean I obviously I got back to my health visitors because I&rsquo;d spoken to them that morning and they told me that he&rsquo;d never actually registered [with a GP] although we&rsquo;d been here by then four years. Yes because it was the beginning of 2005, four years.</p>

<p><strong>Nobody would have known about him feeling depressed then if he hadn&rsquo;t been to see anybody?</strong></p>

<p>No.</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2221, 113, 'Ted''s father had had episodes of depression ever since he suffered post-traumatic stress from the war.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653390');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2222, 113, 'Ted''s father had had episodes of depression ever since he suffered post-traumatic stress from the war.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>It was 25th March 1964 that he died and I think in common with a lot of other people who&rsquo;ve lost people through suicide it is engraved upon your memory. I remember that day, that was the day I remember from my childhood, everything changed on that day, we were just an ordinary family before, my mother my father and the two of us, we went on holiday every year, I struggled with the school work, and there was absolutely nothing abnormal about us, my father worked in a bank, he was a bank clerk. He drove me to school every morning, I was really unaware that he was suffering from very bad depression. There were a few clues in the time leading up to the time he took his life but I was just getting a bit lippy then, I was 12 years old and I really was thinking, &ldquo;Well why did you do that.&rdquo; And I remember him; he&rsquo;d got upset because I beat him at cards. Well that&rsquo;s the wrong way round really isn&rsquo;t it, and I thought, &ldquo;Well why are you getting upset,&rdquo; but it was clearly a symptom of his depression.</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p>And in fact my father&rsquo;s depression as I later discovered, was post traumatic stress, from experiences that he had during the war. So anyway, I went to university, I dropped out, I took lots of drugs, and again you might say that I might not have done that, but on the other hand a lot of other people took drugs in the late 60&rsquo;s early 70&rsquo;s, so I might&rsquo;ve done, I might not have done, if you see what I mean. You can&rsquo;t, you know, say that event A has&hellip;. I certainly don&rsquo;t regret any of it, anyway as regards the death I never talked to anybody about it that knew my father. But I did talk to other people, I talked to, I talked to friends, and I had this story and the story was one my mother told me when she got older, and she told me the story and again she said he&rsquo;d had bad experiences during the war, he got depressed at Christmas 1963, when we were at our relatives, he got more and more depressed, it related to a breakdown he&rsquo;d had after the war in 1946, it was 18 years without depression, then he got depressed, he was having nightmares, he was dreaming about sailors drowning because he was in the navy. He couldn&rsquo;t go into work. Every time he went into work he came back shaking and in a terrible state. She was up all night talking to him. He was hallucinating. And all the time he was just taking me to school, and behaving quite normally.</p></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<p>Some people told us that their relative who had&nbsp;died due to&nbsp;suicide had been diagnosed with bipolar disorder, which used to be called &#39;manic depression&#39;.</p>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tAudio v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2223, 113, 'Jenny''s husband was diagnosed with bipolar disorder just before he died.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653037');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2224, 113, 'Jenny''s husband was diagnosed with bipolar disorder just before he died.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>David, my husband died, he took his own life in March of this year, March 2007, and&nbsp;he jumped from a tower.&nbsp;He was diagnosed with bipolar disorder about five days before he died and what actually happened was that he&rsquo;d got very, very bad.&nbsp;He&rsquo;d been depressed consistently for the previous six months, which was a longer period than he&rsquo;d ever been depressed for before&hellip;</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>Hmm.</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And I think we tried various different drugs.&nbsp;He had been on&nbsp;Seroxat and&nbsp;eventually was taking Mirtazapine.&nbsp;He&rsquo;d also tried some mood disorder drugs like Olanzapine&nbsp;and it just felt like it had been going on for a long time and he was getting very, very desperate.&nbsp;And he had actually talked of suicide and he had written a letter to me probably two or three weeks before he actually died. But he, he confessed the letter to me and we sort of burned it deliberately together, as if to say that, you know, we&rsquo;d talked about it and drawn a line under it and it wasn&rsquo;t going to happen.&nbsp;And he said to me, &ldquo;You know, I&rsquo;d never do it, don&rsquo;t you?&rdquo;&nbsp;And I really believed him.&nbsp;</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>Hmm.</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And maybe he believed it himself at the time, I&rsquo;m not sure. But, anyway, there had been this, this threat of suicide.&nbsp;And as I say, just before it had happened, just before he died he had been diagnosed with bipolar disorder.&nbsp;What had happened was we had, we&rsquo;d got him into a really good hospital in London.&nbsp;We suddenly realised that we had some medical insurance, through David&rsquo;s work, and we weren&rsquo;t aware it would, would cover this kind of hospital treatment.&nbsp;But it did.&nbsp;And he was desperate to go in.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>We got him in there and he was diagnosed with bipolar.&nbsp;And I think neither of us really quite believed it at first because we&rsquo;d lived with David&rsquo;s depression for so long and, I think, my impression certainly of bipolar disorder was the very severe end of it, the manic depression.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>Hmm.</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>
<p>And, and, I think that&rsquo;s what most people understand about, you know, they see the sort of Steven Fry documentary, that kind of thing&nbsp;And you think about people that maybe take a lot of drugs or re-mortgage their house or whatever it is, you know. So it&rsquo;s a sort of stereotypical view of it and it&rsquo;s also the extreme end of it.&nbsp;But this particular expert explained to us that the latest thinking is that there&rsquo;s a whole, a whole sort of spectrum of, a bipolar spectrum, and you can either be, you are either unipolar depressed, what they call unipolar depressed, which is what most people understand, the kind of futility, not wanting to get up in the morning, feeling like life&rsquo;s just not worth it.&nbsp;And that&rsquo;s one end of the scale and the other end is the manic depression.&nbsp;But you can be anywhere between the two.&nbsp;This is, this is the latest thinking.&nbsp;And they said, you know, they thought that David was somewhere on this scale.</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<div>But David was a very sort of unusual person and I think, he was a real perfectionist, and I think he felt that he had to be absolutely 100% better, because, you know, he said,&nbsp;when he said, you know, &ldquo;What if I, what if I don&rsquo;t have it, any highs?&nbsp;What if I don&rsquo;t experience my highs?&rdquo;&nbsp;You know, I think what I&rsquo;m saying is the 50% better wasn&rsquo;t good enough for him.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>Hmm.</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And, and actually the psychiatrist said, he said, &ldquo;Well we have had really creative people in here, we&rsquo;ve had writers,&rdquo; and he said, you know, &ldquo;But we&rsquo;ve, they&rsquo;ve said that, afterwards they haven&rsquo;t been able to write.&rdquo; But he said, &ldquo;They&rsquo;ve been in here, you know, they, long enough that we have tweaked the drugs and with that and therapy and so on we have got them right again.&rdquo;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
So there was, you know, they, they, you know, he was even addressing that concern. You know, we, we can get there with you. But I think David had got to the point where he had, maybe he had lost hope or he just got so exhausted, he got so exhausted by that point that he just thought, &ldquo;No.&rdquo; And maybe that combined with the sort of lack of sleep and so on, maybe that was why.<br />
&nbsp;</div>
</div></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2225, 113, 'Felicity describes what happened when her daughter, Alice, first became ill with bipolar disorder.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653045');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2226, 113, 'Felicity describes what happened when her daughter, Alice, first became ill with bipolar disorder.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>Fine, Alice Duncan was my oldest child, and she was a very lively, bright, pretty girl. You wouldn’t have imagined her being somebody who would suffer from depression. In her teens she got became quite sort of moody, but we didn’t think this was anything abnormal, and when she was 17 when she was doing her mock A’ levels, she became more difficult, and again we just thought this was you know teenage difficulties. Then one day I was at work and I was phoned by her school and she’d walked out of a mock A’ level exam, in a sort of zombie state. I took her to the doctor who said she mustn’t take any more exams. She was quite clearly suffering from depression. She could hardly speak. We had to look after her at home. She went to the local psychiatric hospital that dealt with under 18’s, as an out patient. Then she became psychotic and she’d just hit 18, which meant that the whole structure had changed. She had to be in the adult hospital and that we found incredibly frustrating and sort of wrong. We’d got this whole set up with the psychiatrist that she trusted and so on. Suddenly, just because she was 18, we had to have a whole new lot of people, and that was something that we always felt very annoyed about. And so there she was. She was in the adult psychiatric hospital, she was on various drugs, she was lying with her face to the wall. But then she seemed to get a bit better and they said that it would be alright if she was discharged. That was on the 31<sup>st</sup> July, and she came home.</div>
</section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2227, 113, 'Felicity and Alex talked to Alice about her bipolar disorder. Alice found it hard to accept that she would have to live with it.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653068');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2228, 113, 'Felicity and Alex talked to Alice about her bipolar disorder. Alice found it hard to accept that she would have to live with it.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>Alex was away for one of those weeks and she talked, we talked a great deal and we talked about her illness. I never talked to her about what it was like to be manic. She never talked to her brothers about it either. I&rsquo;d assumed she would have done. She was quite private in that way. But she did talk about how dark it felt you know, and how alone she felt in this depression. And she was also very remorseful about what she&rsquo;d put us through. And of course you couldn&rsquo;t say, &ldquo;Oh don&rsquo;t give it another thought,&rdquo; because it had torn us all apart, and I had tremendously resented the effect that her illness, her manic illness had on her brothers because it had sort of made their lives so ghastly. But she&rsquo;d not been remorseful before, and now she was. And I remember her saying, &ldquo;I wish I could put the clock back&rdquo;, and I said, &ldquo;But, but now we know that you have this illness, you have this problem, but we know how to deal with it.&nbsp;And you can lead a normal life. Read your K. Jamieson, you know it is possible to have a really really good life, as long as you can take the medication and can learn to live with it.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>Anyway, she decided she wanted to go back to Glasgow and I said &ldquo;Please don&rsquo;t, because you know you&rsquo;re still low.&rdquo; And she said, &ldquo;Yes, but I&rsquo;ll be fine and I&rsquo;ve got a lot to do, and I promise you I&rsquo;ll see the psychiatrist right away.&rdquo; She was a very determined person, she always had been absolutely, had her own very strong will and so she went back, and Alex put her on a train. She said to him on the journey to the train that she was finding it very difficult to come to terms with the fact that this was something that she was going to have to live with for the rest of her life.</div></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2229, 113, 'Explains why her daughter Rose became suicidal. Rose was first diagnosed with depression and later with bipolar disorder. Rose lost all hope that she would get better', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653412');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2230, 113, 'Explains why her daughter Rose became suicidal. Rose was first diagnosed with depression and later with bipolar disorder. Rose lost all hope that she would get better', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>Yes. So we&rsquo;re talking about Rose and who died November, 2005. I think probably that the depression that she suffered from had started a long time before. But it wasn&rsquo;t, I didn&rsquo;t recognise the depression she&rsquo;d had on and off for about ten years was the reason for her suicide. She had anorexia badly at fourteen. And she had a complicated teenage life, having affairs with older men. So there was a great imbalance in her character.</p>

<p><strong>Hmm.</strong></p>

<p>But it went with a tremendous verve and huge charisma and just &hellip; she had more friends than anyone I&rsquo;ve ever known and five, six hundred people came to her funeral which at twenty eight is quite something. So it was very complicated to know why it was that this huge imbalance was going on, that in the end led to this absolute despair.</p>

<p>She went to New Zealand in 2000, 1 or 2 and came back in 2003. And in the meantime she&rsquo;d had a serious accident. Someone, a woman knocked her off her bicycle and she landed on her head. And had &hellip; and sustained I think quite bad neck injuries and head injuries. And from that moment on felt that she, she couldn&rsquo;t work. She thought she&rsquo;d got ME. I think she actually had a serious injury.</p>

<p><strong>Hmhm.</strong></p>

<p>And things really went down hill from then.</p>

<p>But suicide was not something I&rsquo;d even considered with her. I now think it was the result of the antidepressants, the mixture. And I have found out since it is quite a possible thing that people do &hellip; it&rsquo;s quite a &hellip;a quick reaction to taking antidepressants is suicide in some people. It can just flick your chemical soup the other way.</p>

<p>I came back and by this time she was seeing a psychiatrist in London but in a very ad hoc basis. And this was at &hellip; a psychiatrist who had she seen her earlier I think might have made a real difference. But she worked for a charitable organisation. And it was too late. She immediately recognised Rose&rsquo;s condition as being bipolar. But it was too late, because Rose had lost all hope by then.</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2231, 113, 'Stephen''s wife had been diagnosed with depression. He thought she probably had bipolar disorder.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653624');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2232, 113, 'Stephen''s wife had been diagnosed with depression. He thought she probably had bipolar disorder.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div><strong>Had she&rsquo;d been ill for many years?</strong>

<p>&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div>Well it&rsquo;s a really good question actually because she had been, in this, in this particular episode of which we thought for quite a long time was just, it was just an isolated incident, we knew was from, well it goes back to November 2004, she died in June 2006, and she was actually diagnosed by the doctor I think sometime in early 2005, February March time, but a friend of hers in October 2005 reminded her of a time back when actually we&rsquo;d first got together, in 1989, and she&rsquo;d been sacked from a job, and she&rsquo;d had a six month period where she had been, when we were told about it, very similar to the way she was in you know in, late on in , in this, this kind of bout, so, and that I mean again looking back on it I&rsquo;ve sort of pieced it all together and really realised in my mind that she was actually bipolar and this wasn&rsquo;t a sort of, this wasn&rsquo;t post natal depression, sort of you know slightly delayed post natal depression or as a result of just you know we&rsquo;d moved out from, from London, had children and she&rsquo;d left her job, and we&rsquo;d been in South Africa for 18 months. She was very reliant on her friends, particularly on her friends, and I think all of that had meant that yes, there were, there was much less, you know, far fewer friends in her life you know, on a day to day basis you know. She was extremely good at keeping in contact with friends, so yeah, I mean I actually found a book when I was clearing up called &ldquo;Depression&rdquo; that I&rsquo;d given her back then, and this was dated October 31st 1989, and after she died her mother told me that three of her aunts on her fathers&rsquo; side had also suffered mental health problems which would&rsquo;ve been nice to have known about beforehand really but you know, hindsight is great isn&rsquo;t it, in all these things?</div></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<p>Stuart said that his ex-partner Anne had symptoms such as headaches and blurred vision. At first, she was diagnosed with depression, and then with generalised anxiety disorder.</p>

<p>The doctors could find no underlying physical cause for her symptoms and eventually diagnosed chronic somatisation (a long-term condition in which a person has physical symptoms that involve more than one part of the body, but no physical cause can be found). The realisation that &lsquo;chronic&rsquo; meant &lsquo;long term&rsquo; really worried her, since she could see no end to her problems.</p>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tAudio v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2233, 113, 'When Anne was told she had chronic somatisation she realised that she might not get relief from her symptoms. This might have made her decide to end her life.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653420');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2234, 113, 'When Anne was told she had chronic somatisation she realised that she might not get relief from her symptoms. This might have made her decide to end her life.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p><strong>Looking back is there anything you think could&rsquo;ve prevented her death?</strong></p>

<p>No, I don&rsquo;t know really. It&rsquo;s difficult to pinpoint any single thing, I think, when she saw the diagnosis of what she had and it said chronic it was somatisation, I think when she saw that it said chronic I think that was a sort of, and chronic meaning long term I think that was really, quite a big deciding factor for her.</p>

<p><strong>Mmm.</strong></p>

<p>But I think it&rsquo;s, I think maybe, I don&rsquo;t know I wasn&rsquo;t there obviously when she was given that diagnosis, I think maybe sometimes the, medical profession when they give these diagnoses they&rsquo;re trying to, do it to sort of help, either the treatment, or to demonstrate that they have some understanding of it, and I think it may be important for them sometimes to say, &ldquo;No we don&rsquo;t actually know everything about this.&rdquo;</p>

<p><strong>Mmm.</strong></p>

<p>Or, &ldquo;What this diagnosis means is x, y and z.&rdquo;</p>

<p><strong>So they actually gave her the diagnosis of chronic somatisation?</strong></p>

<p>Yeah somatisation.</p>

<p><strong>Mmm.</strong></p>

<p>But then we looked it up on the web together, and we saw that it meant chronic and I think that was the thing that really worried her was the long term effects of it and I think she just wanted to see a way out of it and I think that was the thing the fact that she saw that there wouldn&rsquo;t be any, that she perceived that there&rsquo;d never be any rest or a relief from it.</p></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<p>People suggested many other reasons why a member of the family had decided to take their own life.&nbsp;</p>

<p>Some people said that relatives diagnosed with schizophrenia or another mental health problem had been discharged from hospital without adequate followup care.&nbsp;Others said that illegal drugs might have made mental health problems worse or might have even caused the death.&nbsp;</p>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tAudio v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2235, 113, 'Explains why she thinks two of her sons committed suicide. Stephen had paranoid schizophrenia. Both boys took drugs such as cannabis and both had become paranoid.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653174');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2236, 113, 'Explains why she thinks two of her sons committed suicide. Stephen had paranoid schizophrenia. Both boys took drugs such as cannabis and both had become paranoid.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>Right, twelve years ago I lost my first son aged 23, he committed suicide and then last April I lost another son aged 35, who did exactly the same, and committed suicide. It&rsquo;s very very hard. I felt a lot of anger and sadness. I was more angry with my second son for doing what he did as he&rsquo;d always promised that he would never do this to us. But I also understand that it was harder for him, trying to cope without his brother, and I think as time goes on I&rsquo;m beginning to understand more why Stephen had to do what he did. He suffered with a mental illness for ten years, paranoid schizophrenia.</p>

<p><strong>Was this the first one?</strong></p>

<p>The second.</p>

<p><strong>The second one, right.</strong></p>

<p>He found that very hard, he would always say that he talked to his brother, he talked to his grandparents, and towards the end before he died, he felt that he was losing his brother&rsquo;s voice, and I think this was always a danger we were told by the hospital, that if that happened then Stephen could become suicidal, but we didn&rsquo;t find this out until after Stephen had died.</p>

<p><strong>By losing his voice, he used to hear his voice?</strong></p>

<p>Barry used to talk to Stephen, which gave him quite a lot of comfort, and also my parents which were Stephen&rsquo;s grandparents, but Stephen also spoke to what he called a demon, which would tell him to do really bad things. And Stephen said that he was being punished and the demon was taking away Barry&rsquo;s voice. It was getting fainter and fainter and he could no longer hear him, so Stephen deteriorated even more, which I believe actually led to Stephen&rsquo;s suicide. I know that he never came to terms with Barry&rsquo;s death; he blamed him self, as they had actually made a pact to die together, and obviously Stephen didn&rsquo;t go through with that, why, we don&rsquo;t know. And I think Stephen carried an awful lot of guilt after Barry died.</p>

<p><strong>Why did, why did Barry decide to die?</strong></p>

<p>Barry had a lot of problems, they both got into drugs quite badly in their teenage years, which I think didn&rsquo;t help their problems, because Cannabis does cause paranoia and both my sons suffered with paranoia, although Barry was never diagnosed as being with schizophrenia.&nbsp;</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2237, 113, 'Helen''s daughter Charlotte often felt depressed. She took cannabis and heroin and died of a heroin overdose.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653113');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2238, 113, 'Helen''s daughter Charlotte often felt depressed. She took cannabis and heroin and died of a heroin overdose.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>My daughter died three years ago of a heroin overdose. She had mental health issues and drug problems, and she&rsquo;d, she&rsquo;d been unwell for twelve years. Her problems started when she was about fifteen or sixteen, when she first used cannabis. She had a psychosis, a small one which we didn&rsquo;t realise what was going on at the time. Didn&rsquo;t really understand what she was saying. We thought she was distressed because we&rsquo;d recently been divorced, and well not recently, it was about five years after. We had no idea that cannabis could cause psychosis, didn&rsquo;t even know she was smoking it at the time. And then it sort of went away, and she had depression, her father took her to the doctor, he said it was normal sort of adolescent growing up; she used to sort of sit on the pavement outside the school and cry. And, then it seemed to pass a bit, and she did her A&rsquo; levels. And then she went to do a foundation year in Art.</div>

<div><br />
And Charlotte only really had to smoke a very small amount of cannabis and it made her very ill, but she, she then was, at the end of that year, was admitted to a psychiatric hospital, for about six weeks and they wanted her to stay longer but she left, of her own will, and then she started a degree course in October, but she didn&rsquo;t make it to Christmas because she started hearing voices and was really not very well. And I said to her at the time, &ldquo;Leave, Charlotte&rdquo;, because I thought, I still thought at that time that she couldn&rsquo;t cope with it because after what had happened to her she needed a bit of a rest, but she was still using drugs, so then she came to live with me, and she, we, she was under a psychiatrist, and she went to, and I don&rsquo;t know who she was now, but I think she was a nurse, a psychiatric nurse, and we used to go together, and we used to have to sort of fill in forms and. And do diagrams about how she felt about herself and how I viewed her, and how she viewed me. And she went there for quite a long time, and then they, they decided it would be good for her to go to psychotherapy. And she went for a very long time to psychotherapy. And she told me for one year in that time she didn&rsquo;t use drugs at all, but she didn&rsquo;t feel any better, she felt just as bad. She, she, she never stopped having depressions.</div></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<p>Amanda thought sexual abuse might have been a factor in her son&rsquo;s death.</p>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2239, 113, 'Two and a half months before he died, Amanda discovered that her son had been sexually abused. She thought this might have contributed to his death.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653089');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2240, 113, 'Two and a half months before he died, Amanda discovered that her son had been sexually abused. She thought this might have contributed to his death.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>Well I suppose we&rsquo;ll start with the day that he actually died, that was twenty months ago and I was at work and I was asked to go to an office, to find a policeman there to inform me that my son had died, jumping, and, that&rsquo;s when the horror really began.&nbsp;But of course the story starts much earlier than that, it starts with realising that your son isn&rsquo;t happy, that things aren&rsquo;t right and not being able to make sense of it.&nbsp;One thing, from my point of view, which other people maybe who&rsquo;ve had a bereavement of a similar type maybe haven&rsquo;t been able to make quite as much sense of it; I think everybody who&rsquo;s lost a child will, in this manner, will have spent a long time trying to work out how much they might be at, if they&rsquo;re being to blame.&nbsp;For Lori we had a reason, we discovered before he&rsquo;d, two, two and a half months before he died, that he&rsquo;d been sexually abused, so that, there is some sense, we can make sense of it, but that, just having that bit doesn&rsquo;t explain it all.</div></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<div>
<p>Ann said that she thought her friend felt that she was becoming a burden due to her chronic fatigue syndrome and found it hard to deal with.</p>

<div class=''vbox tText v4x3 hideit''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2241, 113, 'Ann''s friend had chronic fatigue syndrome. Doctors said they couldn''t help her and she felt she was becoming a burden.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''textOnly''><div>Text only<div><img src=''/img/book.png''/></div><span>Read below</span></div></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2242, 113, 'Ann''s friend had chronic fatigue syndrome. Doctors said they couldn''t help her and she felt she was becoming a burden.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>HIDE TEXT</div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p><strong>At the time that she died was she having any contact with health professionals at that time?</strong></p>

<p>No, no. And mainly because we had, you know, seen sort of the top people to do with chronic fatigue syndrome. And they&rsquo;d said there was, you know, they were very sorry; even in a letter from the specialist saying you know he was really sorry but at the moment in time there was nothing you know that they could do to offer any help.</p>

<p><strong>Hmm.</strong></p>

<p>And also she had some; the antidepressants drugs are quite suitable for you know chronic fatigue syndrome. And she had tried those previously but they actually made her worse.</p>

<p><strong>Oh.</strong></p>

<p>So there hadn&rsquo;t been any foreseeable solution to her difficulty. And she certainly felt that she was becoming a burden, and that I was having to do everything. And you know if somebody perceives it like that, you know, I just thought well, some days she&rsquo;s good, some days she&rsquo;s bad and we just go with it.</p>

<p><strong>Hmm.</strong></p>

<p>But she actually couldn&rsquo;t deal with that.</p></section></div>
</div>
  

<p>One woman we talked to said that her father had decided to&nbsp;die by&nbsp;suicide at the age of 78 because he had incurable stomach cancer. For at least 20 years he had told his children that did not want a &#39;lingering death&#39;. His wife had died and he was very lonely.</p>

<p>Another woman told us about how her family helped her father to end his own life by assisted suicide (euthanasia) in Switzerland. He had mainly lost his motor neurone function and wantedan assisted suicide.</p>
</div>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2243, 113, 'She accepted her father''s decision to take his own life. He had incurable stomach cancer and did not want to return to hospital.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653727');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2244, 113, 'She accepted her father''s decision to take his own life. He had incurable stomach cancer and did not want to return to hospital.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>My father decided to commit suicide when he was 78 years old. I think there&rsquo;s quite a history to that decision though and the fact is that he was a very larger than life man, he was a very vibrant, active man, for all those years, he&rsquo;d had a wonderful life as far as he was concerned, and as far as we were concerned. He&rsquo;d done a lot of travelling, he was full of life and laughter and had given us a very secure and wonderful family life really, we always felt that there was happiness in the home. However when he was 18 and he was learning to fly, he joined the air force at that stage, he had a terrible accident, and he had to spend a year and a half in hospital when he was 18, and I think that gives us some of the background as to why, when he was 78 and was diagnosed with stomach cancer for the second time he decided that he didn&rsquo;t want a lingering death, he&rsquo;d already spent several years of his life being an invalid, and depending on other people, so that at 78 when he couldn&rsquo;t foresee living much longer, he decided that he&rsquo;d terminate his life.</p>

<p><br />
At that stage, I mean also in his particular circumstances at the time, he&rsquo;d been married to my mother for 50 years, and she had died in 1999 which was 2 years before he committed suicide and I think that was a factor. He&rsquo;d obviously become deeply lonely.</p>

<p><br />
Well he was in a lot of pain, and really he&rsquo;d, he&rsquo;d just had an aversion to hospitals having spent two years when he was younger in hospital, he didn&rsquo;t ever want to spend time in hospital again I don&rsquo;t think. So he made the decision to take his own life. I think the difficulty with that was that he chose quite a violent way to commit suicide, so therefore the friends didn&rsquo;t understand why he&rsquo;d chosen to go over a cliff, however we as his children, three of us, had almost been prepared by him, because he said if he ever had a terminal illness he would not want his death to be lingering or have extended pain in any way. He&rsquo;d always said he&rsquo;d go quickly and always said it would be over this particular cliff so it sounds strange but, but we weren&rsquo;t as shocked by the way he chose to die, and also on the previous evening he&rsquo;d actually phoned us all up, and more or less told us that he had decided not to go on with his life. I think one of the things why we all accepted it so easily was that you know we felt that he&rsquo;d had such a full life.</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tText v4x3 hideit''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2245, 113, 'Gillian''s father asked Dignitas to help him die in a dignified manner.  He died a peaceful death in Zurich with his wife and two children by his bedside.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''textOnly''><div>Text only<div><img src=''/img/book.png''/></div><span>Read below</span></div></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2246, 113, 'Gillian''s father asked Dignitas to help him die in a dignified manner.  He died a peaceful death in Zurich with his wife and two children by his bedside.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>HIDE TEXT</div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p><strong>So if you would start by telling me the full story from the beginning, please.</strong></p>

<p>My father was in a nursing home as he had mainly lost his motor neurone function, and was getting progressively worse, whilst unfortunately being of completely sound mind. And he was aged 84. And all my life my parents had brought me up to believe in the right to terminate your life when you decided to. And my father asked me, well, when he was, still lived at home, he&rsquo;d asked me to get details of Dignitas, based in Switzerland, because they were the only organisation and country we knew of who would help people end their lives.</p>

<p>And so using Google I found out Dignitas&rsquo;s address and wrote to them. And they sent us information on how to join. It&rsquo;s a charity in Switzerland, so it&rsquo;s all by way of donations, and you pay to be a member. And at the time that we got the details my father did nothing further.&nbsp; It was then just before he went in the home that he became a member of Dignitas. And the home was a private home. It&rsquo;s not how my father had wished to spend the money he&rsquo;d saved to pass on to his children. Physically he was in a right state and very miserable. My brother lives in the south of France and so was having to come over every month to see my parents. He&rsquo;d stay for 24 hours. My partner and I would see my father two to three times a week in the home. And he was very very unhappy. Dignitas will only help people who have a terminal illness. And unfortunately my father wasn&rsquo;t actually diagnosed with motor neurone and so did not have a terminal illness, and was just getting progressively worse. So we wrote to Dignitas, well, I wrote to Dignitas. My mother, although she believes strongly in the right to terminate your life, wasn&rsquo;t very cooperative at this stage. She&rsquo;d been married to my father for sixty-two years.&nbsp; It was very difficult for her and she wasn&rsquo;t ready, and therefore why should he be ready to leave her? And although she realised intellectually this was ridiculous and his position was completely untenable, nonetheless she did sort of pursue that line of thinking for some time. So in the meantime I was the one who sort of led the way.</p>

<p>Wrote to Dignitas. They wrote back. They wanted copies of doctors&rsquo; letters. And I had to speak to his doctor a number of times. She had had a couple of conversations with my father and he&rsquo;d made it quite plain that he believed in euthanasia. And she was not uncooperative and she was not unsympathetic. Having said that, and, and, you know, when she could, when she could help she was prompt in dealing with it, it&rsquo;s just that, you know, she wouldn&rsquo;t go out of her way. Which is fair enough. But, you know, seemed like a nice person.</p>

<p>And so this process took months and they wrote back and said, &ldquo;No.&rdquo; Which was just a huge blow to all of us, particularly my father, who was by this time quite desperate.&nbsp; And so we wrote, and the family wrote, in fact my mother wrote most of it. And it was basically a begging letter. Their tenet is that they help people end their lives with dignity and that&rsquo;s all my father was asking. And we, we basically said in the letter, &ldquo;He&rsquo;s 84 years old. You know, he&rsquo;s going to die. Help him die well.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>

<p>And I can&rsquo;t tell you how amazing it was to get an email giving us the green light. And they&rsquo;re a fantastic organisation. All the people we dealt with were fantastic. And they write and they say, you know, &ldquo;You&rsquo;ve got the green light. You don&rsquo;t have to do it now, because you know we&rsquo;ll help. Or you can come and visit and just see. Or, you know, you can come and do it.&rdquo; Well, there was no question that we had to go and do it.&nbsp; And what they did was give you something to drink&nbsp;apparently it tasted horrible, and then you fell asleep. You would then go into a coma. You would then die. And how long it took all depended on how old you were and the state of your functions and.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
And then about this time, it was possible to basically do it in 24 hours, to arrive on Sunday and have it done Monday morning. And they have a flat where they do it. And my father needed 24-hour care. He was unable to do anything for himself. He was by that time unable to even feed himself. And then there was something in the press, because they got a lot, lot of bad press, Dignitas, and they were told that if people were going to drink, they had to be in the country for at least four days, which was a huge blow, huge blow, because, you know, it was difficult. We, we weren&rsquo;t sure how we&rsquo;d manage for 24 hours without help. But Dignitas came back and said that it was okay, they&rsquo;d do another method. Which is basically by gassing, and as long as he was able to pull a gas mask over his face. Nicer for the patient apparently, much worse for the witnesses. So they would suggest that my mother and I did not witness it and left the room. Now I never told my mother this. I spoke to my father about it and he said, &ldquo;That&rsquo;s fine&rdquo; he&rsquo;s happy to do it that way. And then, because my father was in such a bad way, we hired a private jet to travel over to Switzerland. And we&rsquo;d booked a hotel for the night. And it was all arranged for the Sunday. And the Wednesday before, they phoned and said, &ldquo;No. Because they won&rsquo;t let us do gas any more. You have to stay for four days.&rdquo; So I rang my brother and we rearranged everything.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
And I rang Dignitas and said, &ldquo;You&rsquo;ll have to find my father an old people&rsquo;s home. We can&rsquo;t take care of him.&rdquo; And, and it was interesting, because they were surprised. They said, &ldquo;Well, you know, you&rsquo;re coming mob-handed. Surely you&rsquo;ll be able to cope.&rdquo; And I said, you know, &ldquo;You don&rsquo;t understand. My father is completely helpless. My mother is 83. My brother is nearly 60.&rdquo; And I had just been, I&rsquo;d been taken ill and had a minor operation the day before, two days before we flew out on the Friday, by general anaesthetic. So I said, you know, &ldquo;This, we can&rsquo;t do this.&rdquo; Now in Switzerland you have to be referred to an old people&rsquo;s home by a doctor. And what was going to happen was on the Sunday we were going to go straight from the airport to a doctor, a Dignitas doctor, who would then interview my father to make sure he wasn&rsquo;t bonkers and that he wasn&rsquo;t being pressurised by his family. Then he&rsquo;d refer him to a home. So we&rsquo;d take him straight to the home. He&rsquo;d then stay there until the Wednesday morning. And we&rsquo;d go to a hotel.&nbsp; And then we&rsquo;d all go to the Dignitas flat outside Zurich and Dad would drink the potion and we&rsquo;d stay with him.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
So I mean amazing people, amazing people. So we flew out on a private jet and we had champagne on the way over. And we went straight to the doctor&rsquo;s. And fantastic taxi driver, with one of those wheelchair lifts, who realised what we were there for and was very nice about it. Fantastic doctor, and because my father was in a wheelchair, he went and sat in the taxi and we went into his home. And he spent about an hour with Dad. And then we went to the home, and we really just sort of, it was quite late by then, we just left him there in the care of the deputy manager. We went to the hotel, had dinner, went to bed. Next day we went back, having changed hotel, and Dad was, was a bit unhappy. He&rsquo;d had a bad night&rsquo;s sleep. The home was just spotless and the people were fantastic and the food was brilliant and he had a really happy three days there. And so did we. We spent every morning with him for about three hours, and then we&rsquo;d go back to the hotel, have lunch, have a rest, and go back and spend two or three hours with him in the afternoon. And we, you know, we chatted about memories and things and nothing and politics and it was the first time we&rsquo;d spent time together as a family for years. And it was really nice. And then the people at the nursing home realised what we were up to and, and actually approved. And they were just worried we were going to do it there. And once they knew we weren&rsquo;t, they couldn&rsquo;t do enough for us. Brilliant people. They cried when we left.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
So we got a taxi and we went to this flat. And it&rsquo;s in a commercial area outside Zurich. And the two people who met us were Erica and her husband. And Erica and I had corresponded with and talked to a lot. And they were the most fantastic people. And they filmed my father three times. They asked him in front of us if this is what he really wanted. He said, &ldquo;Yes, very much.&rdquo; And they film him, they, we then go in the back bedroom, bit of classical music on. And he drank his potion in his two-handled mug that we&rsquo;ve taken with, dragged to Switzerland with us, and, and just went, &ldquo;Well, that wasn&rsquo;t too bad then&rdquo; and put it down on the table. And then we were all, we were all sort of holding hands and holding his hand. And we were sitting there for about a minute and he, he said, and I think he was going to say, &ldquo;It&rsquo;s not working yet&rdquo; but actually he just went, &ldquo;Oh&rdquo; and just fell asleep very suddenly. And we stayed with him for about five minutes and then we left the room. All sort of cried a bit and hugged each other and had another bloody cup of tea.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
And Dad fell into a coma and actually died within an hour. And it was, it was really nice, it was really nice. And then they call the police. And you can get the nice guys or the nasty guys, depending, you know. We got the nice guys. And they interview you and just say, you know, &ldquo;Did he do it of his own accord?&rdquo; And, &ldquo;Yes, he did. And here&rsquo;s his passport.&rdquo; And then they call, I don&rsquo;t know, the other people, and then finally the undertaker arrives. So we got there at 9 in the morning and we left at 1 o&rsquo;clock in the afternoon. And in fact we saw him in his coffin, which is not how I&rsquo;ll remember him. And it was a, you know, I&rsquo;ll remember him other ways. But it was the best, it was the best thing. Mum and I went back to the airport and we had a bottle of champagne on the flight back, and we felt very smug and we thought the family had done good.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
<strong>So going, going right back.</strong><br />
&nbsp;<br />
Yes.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
<strong>When your father first intimated that this was something that he would like to do, was it a surprise to you at all?</strong><br />
&nbsp;<br />
Not at all, not at all. We&rsquo;d always talked about euthanasia as a family. We all approved of it. There was no question. And he&rsquo;d been ill for quite a long time and had actually tried to commit suicide by taking paracetamol, although I had already told him not to because it wouldn&rsquo;t work. But he didn&rsquo;t listen to me. So it was absolutely no surprise at all.</p></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<p>For more information on assisted dying see Dignity in Dying&rsquo;s website (&lsquo;<a href="https://www.hexi.ox.ac.uk/bereavement-due-suicide/resources-information" target="_blank">Resources and Information</a>&rsquo;).</p>

<p>Lynne thought that perhaps her mother&rsquo;s mental health problems became much worse when her doctor rapidly reduced the medicines she was having. Her mother had recently moved house and was seeing a new GP, which made the situation more difficult.</p>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2247, 113, 'Lynne tried to make sense of what had happened. She wondered if her mother''s psychotropic medicines had been withdrawn too quickly.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653487');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2248, 113, 'Lynne tried to make sense of what had happened. She wondered if her mother''s psychotropic medicines had been withdrawn too quickly.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>No, obviously you kind of try and make sense of what happened and I think that&rsquo;s one of the hardest things for, for families of people who have committed suicide is trying to make sense of the whole process and just what went wrong and what could&rsquo;ve been done, but I, I have my own, I suppose the way I&rsquo;ve made sense of that is that this was quite a long time ago, and Mum was on quite heavy medication at that time and for some reason just before Mum and Dad moved they started cutting down her medication and cutting it down quite quickly. And I think that was probably at a time where people didn&rsquo;t know so much about withdrawing people from psychotropic medication. And I just wonder whether some of the things, because she started talking for the first time that she was hearing voices and people, somebody was telling her to kill herself and things, and that had just never ever been part of her problems ever before, and that at that period of time was she was just behaving very very differently, and I suppose the way I&rsquo;ve kind of tried to make sense of that in my own mind is that maybe that was associated with the cut in her medication. But also during that time because they&rsquo;d moved, probably about 20 miles away, she was almost between GP&rsquo;s, so she&rsquo;d left her GP who was a very traditional family GP and he&rsquo;d known my mother&rsquo;s parents and he&rsquo;d known my mother and he&rsquo;d, you know was at the hospital when I was born so he knew us as a family, so I think she&rsquo;d gone from a, a fairly supportive environment that, that all of her treatment was really handled by the GP, there wasn&rsquo;t an extended team, to living in a new town, where she had lost that contact.</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p><strong>Mm.</strong></p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p>And was under a new GP who was part of quite a large practice, and so I think the time at which her medication was being cut she was also in transition because they&rsquo;d moved house, between health care teams as well, although during that time she was still being seen by her psychiatrist and she did have an appointment to go up to see the psychiatrist a few days before she killed herself so she had been for that appointment. And the way my father recounted that was that she did tell the psychiatrist that she was feeling suicidal, but I&rsquo;m not sure whether he just didn&rsquo;t believe her or, or what, but he actually, she had her appointment with the psychiatrist on the Friday, and she killed herself on the Sunday. So&hellip;.</p></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<div>
<p>Some of the people we talked to believed that their relative had not intended to take their life.</p>

<div class=''vbox tAudio v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2249, 113, 'Brenda''s son was very depressed at times and said he felt suicidal. She was told he had bipolar disorder.  He took an overdose but Brenda believes he just wanted “respite”.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653246');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2250, 113, 'Brenda''s son was very depressed at times and said he felt suicidal. She was told he had bipolar disorder.  He took an overdose but Brenda believes he just wanted “respite”.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>He then got a job in an old people&rsquo;s home and he was there for two years, caring for old people, looking after them, doing all the things one has to do. But unfortunately there was an awful lot of death, obviously because they were elderly, and so one day he&rsquo;d come home and the next day he&rsquo;d go back and somebody had passed away. And I didn&rsquo;t feel this was right for my son. He became very, very depressive, very, very low, really, really down. We took him to the doctor and they prescribed some medication and he immediately went on that. He then hit rock bottom and then ended up in hospital. He was in hospital for six months. On hindsight I wish he had never have gone now because I feel that it wasn&rsquo;t the right place for him but at the time, not being medical, I just thought this would be the best thing for him to do. Both my husband and I supported him, we visited him, but it used to break my heart every time I used to leave the hospital and come away leaving him behind and thinking &lsquo;it&rsquo;s almost like a punishment there, he&rsquo;s just having medication and lying in a bed&rsquo;.</p>

<p>He came home, leveled out again, went back to work, and then it happened again, very, very dark despair. At this time he was very suicidal and he used to say, &ldquo;I can&rsquo;t go on like this any more mum, I just can&rsquo;t live each day like this&rdquo;. And I used to say, &ldquo;Why? What do you mean?&rdquo; And he said, &ldquo;I feel very suicidal&rdquo;. And I used to ask him, &ldquo;How? Why? What were you going to do? How, which way, what were your thoughts? What, you know, how were you going to do it?&rdquo; I just had no idea. And he said, &ldquo;I just want to just take a lot of tablets and go to sleep&rdquo;.</p>

<p><strong>Yes. And the day you came home when he told you he&rsquo;d taken the overdose, what kind of state was he in having taken all of those tablets?</strong></p>

<p>Very, very calm. He walked down the stairs and he sat on a chair. He sat just here in the kitchen, and he said, &ldquo;Mum, sorry Mum I&rsquo;ve taken an overdose&rdquo;. And I suppose immediately I thought it was, probably, I don&rsquo;t know, a couple of dozen tablets or just a handful of tablets. I had no idea of doing anything like that myself. I remember running upstairs and just looking at the bathroom floor and just seeing the whole of the bathroom strewn with tablet packets and wondering for a split second where on earth have they all come from. But I realised they were drugs that had been left in the drawer of myself and my husband&rsquo;s which had been there. We had no worry at all at any time that this was going to, that he would do that because he used to say, &ldquo;Although I have thoughts, although this is what I might do Mum I&rsquo;d never do it, I&rsquo;m too frightened.&rdquo;</p>

<p>And he was a boy that, he wasn&rsquo;t streetwise, he wasn&rsquo;t tough, he would, I still don&rsquo;t know. I think he just wanted a respite that day. I truly believe that it was a respite rather than anything because I don&rsquo;t think he knew, because we were so close, the devastation that he&rsquo;d leave behind for myself and my husband, he was a boy that would never really, when he was levelled out, would ever hurt us. Yes we would have bad days as I&rsquo;ve said, but he wouldn&rsquo;t remember them and wouldn&rsquo;t remember that&rsquo;s what he&rsquo;d done or what he&rsquo;d said. So I truly believe that he had no idea that he was going to die really.</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2251, 113, 'Robina was suffering “horrific domestic violence”. Jasvinder is convinced that her sister did not mean to kill herself. She believes that Robina’s actions were a cry for help', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653387');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2252, 113, 'Robina was suffering “horrific domestic violence”. Jasvinder is convinced that her sister did not mean to kill herself. She believes that Robina’s actions were a cry for help', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>Robina in her love marriage, she still was in this position of having to consider the families honour, izzat, we call it, and that really meant her decision making was impacted by this concept and one of the things she used to tell me was that she was suffering horrific domestic violence in her relationship. She was suffering physically and certainly emotionally, mentally. I had witnessed bruises and I remember saying to her at the time, &ldquo;Leave him.&rdquo; And she used to say to me, &ldquo;It&rsquo;s easy for you to say that, because you don&rsquo;t have to think about Mum, Dad, the community and what people think.&rdquo; And I used to say, &ldquo;But he&rsquo;s hurting you, I&rsquo;ll take you in, leave him.&rdquo; But she couldn&rsquo;t and she really was making the point that in my position of being disowned by my family, I didn&rsquo;t have the authority to give her that advice. So I always asked her to go back to Mum, tell Mum, to tell Dad about the situation. Which she did and my Mother&rsquo;s response was always, as it was with my other sisters; I&rsquo;d witnessed this as a child, to go back, to make the marriage work for the sake of honour, for the sake of the family&rsquo;s honour.</p>

<p><br />
So I, I don&rsquo;t believe that she intended to kill herself, I don&rsquo;t believe that. You know, I&rsquo;m not an expert in suicide, and maybe some people make that decision and plan it or don&rsquo;t, but I don&rsquo;t believe she did, I believe there was so many attempts, direct attempts in terms of crying out for help, I still believe that, and at the point where there could&rsquo;ve been the intervention, she wasn&rsquo;t saved and okay, it maybe my role to help her, and I did, but I never had any power because of my position of being disowned, and they did, they being the family and the community. And ultimately it would&rsquo;ve, it was them she would&rsquo;ve listened to, and clearly there&rsquo;s evidence it wasn&rsquo;t me.</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2253, 113, 'Kate is sure that her daughters did not mean to kill themselves. Too much alcohol and other factors led to the tragic events.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653140');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2254, 113, 'Kate is sure that her daughters did not mean to kill themselves. Too much alcohol and other factors led to the tragic events.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>She [Izzy] worked with her cousin and he&rsquo;s in his forties. And he told us that he phoned her on the Thursday and said to Izzy that one of her colleagues, a chap had gassed himself in the car, carbon monoxide poisoning on the Monday. And he had an argument with his wife and he had a three month old son and he just went off and did this. And, and Izzy was so upset, so upset. And when she found this out, she mentioned it and Anna said that she mentioned it a few times when they were sitting up drinking, how upset she was over this colleague&rsquo;s death. And so I think all of this going on and Izzy was very, very drunk.</div>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<div><strong>Hmm.</strong></div>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<div>And the post mortem &hellip; she had&nbsp;two and half times over legal limit alcohol.</div>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<div><strong>Hmm.</strong></div>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<div>And I, I kept saying she wouldn&rsquo;t have done this, she was drunk. She didn&rsquo;t mean to do it. She keeps saying sorry.</div>

<div><br />
Izzy was very, very drunk. She didn&rsquo;t know what she was doing. It was a spur of the moment thing. She left no note. She probably just wanted to go to sleep or she was trying. I don&rsquo;t &hellip; we never know, we never know. But I knew that she didn&rsquo;t want to do it. Anna did leave a note. She left a 28 page note. And it was the break-up of her relationship with her boyfriend and the death of Izzy. But what finally, finally as she said was the last straw was somebody stole her computer from her flat, that was on Christmas day. And she had all her pictures and all her work and everything and she said somebody broke &hellip; not broke in but had keys and went into her flat and took her computer. And she said it was all my lifetime, all my everything was on that computer.</div></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tText v4x3 hideit''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2255, 113, 'Patricia does not think her husband meant to kill himself. She describes the second “incident” and says that each time Andrew harmed himself “the act was the catharsis”.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''textOnly''><div>Text only<div><img src=''/img/book.png''/></div><span>Read below</span></div></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2256, 113, 'Patricia does not think her husband meant to kill himself. She describes the second “incident” and says that each time Andrew harmed himself “the act was the catharsis”.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>HIDE TEXT</div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div style="line-height'' normal">But the second incident that I was aware of that indicated his depressive illness, underlying depressive illness, was when he was brought home by our GP on one occasion covered with what looked like, I don&rsquo;t know, white stuff all down the front of his suit, shirt, trousers and it was the aspirin he&rsquo;d vomited up.&nbsp;He had taken an overdose and then gone straight to the GP.&nbsp;So some people would say that was an attempt to kill himself.&nbsp;I would say it wasn&rsquo;t because you don&rsquo;t tend to take a whacking overdose and then go and tell a medic, if you intend to kill yourself.&nbsp;I think &hellip; I&rsquo;ve learned a lot in the last ten years that I&rsquo;ve worked, building Survivors of Bereavement by Suicide.&nbsp;I&rsquo;ve met a lot of other people.&nbsp;I&rsquo;ve met a lot of professionals and I&rsquo;ve read an awful lot.</div>

<div style="line-height'' normal">&nbsp;</div>

<div style="line-height'' normal"><strong>Hmm.</strong></div>

<div style="line-height'' normal">&nbsp;</div>

<div style="line-height'' normal">And in my opinion and also my experience of my husband, my first hand observations, for Andrew, the act was the catharsis.&nbsp;Followed almost immediately with the thought, &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t want to die&rdquo; because on other occasions that I&rsquo;ll talk about he also went himself &hellip; and he wasn&rsquo;t found &hellip; he went and sought medical help for what he&rsquo;d done.</div></section></div>
</div>
  

<div>
<p>Some people remained perplexed and had no idea why the suicide had taken place.</p>

<p>Michael did not know why his friend killed himself. He was not aware that he was unhappy.</p>

<p>Linda had no idea that her daughter was feeling unhappy or depressed when she took her own life. Bob said his son &#39;concealed his worries&#39; and thought that perhaps the attitude that &#39;men don&rsquo;t cry&#39; was the reason.</p>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tText v4x3 hideit''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2257, 113, 'Linda can think of no reason for her daughter’s suicide. Chloe’s death shocked the family completely.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''textOnly''><div>Text only<div><img src=''/img/book.png''/></div><span>Read below</span></div></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2258, 113, 'Linda can think of no reason for her daughter’s suicide. Chloe’s death shocked the family completely.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>HIDE TEXT</div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>My daughter, Chloe, was 13. She was off school sick for a couple of days, just with stomach ache, so we&rsquo;d, we&rsquo;d left her at home. And on the Tuesday, me and my husband, we kept phoning home to make sure she was OK, and I finished early, I finished work at 12 and came home to check her. And she was getting better. She was very tired because she&rsquo;d been up most of the night being ill &hellip;</p>

<p><strong>OK.</strong></p>

<p>But we&rsquo;d said, &ldquo;You can stay at home again tomorrow, because you&rsquo;re tired.&rdquo; And she said, &ldquo;Yeah, but will you not phone me,&rdquo; because like me and my husband kept phoning her, to make sure, she was OK. And she said that she couldn&rsquo;t sleep because we kept phoning. So, the next day we went to work, and we didn&rsquo;t phone her. Didn&rsquo;t phone until, I phoned her about 11 o&rsquo;clock thinking that she would have had a good sleep and there was no answer so I just started to think, oh, you know, she&rsquo;s asleep. Or she&rsquo;s, she&rsquo; on the computer or something like that, she just can&rsquo;t get to the phone. But then I started to ring sort of every hour and then every half an hour&hellip; until about half three. And I came home, rushed home, just thinking like, &ldquo;Oh, she must be ill.&rdquo; And when I got home, walked in the door and [found her].</p>

<p><strong>Did she leave any notes or anything?</strong></p>

<p>No, nothing at all. But then I used to do things like check the computer to see if she&rsquo;d left anything on there or if there were any clues and then I remember when the phone bill came I rang up to see if there&rsquo;d been any phone calls made sort of &hellip; in the day when we weren&rsquo;t there. But, I don&rsquo;t know, after a while I think I just sort of accepted that there wasn&rsquo;t any reason that I can think of, just, I think that she was just, must have been really depressed and just alone in herself and she couldn&rsquo;t see any other way really.</p>

<p><strong>Had she ever told you that she was feeling depressed or &hellip;</strong></p>

<p>No.</p>

<p><strong>So she&rsquo;s never been to the doctor for that sort of thing?</strong></p>

<p>No. So it was a complete shock.</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2259, 113, 'Bob had no idea that his son, Darren, was depressed. Bob thinks that Darren may have found it hard to express his emotions because of the British attitude that “men don’t cry”.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878653345');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2260, 113, 'Bob had no idea that his son, Darren, was depressed. Bob thinks that Darren may have found it hard to express his emotions because of the British attitude that “men don’t cry”.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>Our son Darren, he was 19, died on 31st August 1998. He died by suicide whilst on holiday in France. We didn&rsquo;t know he was depressed, he&rsquo;d been a full time student at college doing A&rsquo; levels, he, he started that in 1997, and by the time 1998 came he&rsquo;d had finished with all of them. Because of one thing or another and he&rsquo;d been; but he got himself a full time job and some time he came home, he used to come home feeling a bit, look a bit down and I spoke to him, but we just took it as the normal teenage; going from education into you know full time employment. But he had a good job, he was at minor supervisory level for a local building supply firm, and well I gave him every chance to talk, but he didn&rsquo;t seem to want to. In August he&rsquo;d arranged several things to do, he&rsquo;d done a bungee jump in 1997 and he, he enjoyed it so much he wanted his mates at work to do it as well, and they, he had set it all up for the whole group of them to go and he&rsquo;d paid for them and he was going, pay for it. And it was going be the Saturday he came back from holiday so he had plans, and he went away, and then it was the August bank holiday Monday, I was, I was at work in the evening and my wife Lynda got the call and that it one of his friends he was with, he&rsquo;d died by suicide, and Lynda had to phone me up at work. Got home as best I could and, and that&rsquo;s when our journey started.</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p>One of the reasons we think might be behind Darren [and what happened] is the &ldquo;Men don&rsquo;t cry&rdquo; attitude, and men, men don&rsquo;t show emotions. He probably held it in, all his emotions in too much, he didn&rsquo;t let it out. And all I would say is, men do cry, and men are allowed to cry, and we have emotions, we should show &lsquo;em and I&rsquo;ve cried, I&rsquo;ve cried on TV as well, national TV I did some interviews with various programmes and yeah, men do cry.</p></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<p><em>If you or someone else is in immediate danger, call 999 or attend A&amp;E right away. If you or someone else needs urgent help for mental health, call 111 or access NHS 111 online at&nbsp;<a href="https://111.nhs.uk/" target="_blank">111.nhs.uk</a>&nbsp;for help.&nbsp;</em></p>

<p><em>If you or someone you know is struggling, help and support are available. See NHS mental health and &#39;<a href="https://www.hexi.ox.ac.uk/bereavement-due-suicide/resources-information" target="_blank">Resources and Information</a>&#39; for more, including help and resources for people bereaved by suicide.</em></p>

<div>Last reviewed: July 2025.</div>

<div>Last updated: October 2012.</div>
</div>
</div></div><p>Copyright &copy; 2024 University of Oxford. All rights reserved.</p>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO articles (id, subcategory_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (114, 114, 'Knife or blunt instrument-related death', '<h1>Bereavement due to traumatic death</h1>
      <h2>Knife or blunt instrument-related death</h2><div class=''content''><p>Learning that a friend or relative has been murdered is deeply shocking (see &#39;<a href="https://hexi.ox.ac.uk/bereavement-due-traumatic-death/changing-emotions-and-physical-reactions" target="_blank">Changing emotions and physical reactions</a>&#39;).</p>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878660625');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2261, 114, 'Experiences with bereavement for traumatic death by knife or blunt instrument', '<p>Some of the people we talked to described what happened after a relative or friend had been stabbed with a knife or hit with a blunt instrument by a complete stranger or by someone they hardly knew.</p>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2262, 114, 'Westley was stabbed by a stranger. He had asked the other man to wait his turn while getting money from a cash machine. Ann heard the news from a family friend over the telephone.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878660625');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2263, 114, 'Westley was stabbed by a stranger. He had asked the other man to wait his turn while getting money from a cash machine. Ann heard the news from a family friend over the telephone.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>Yes, Westley, on the 12<sup>th</sup> September 2005 had actually decided to spend the day with his older brother, and had gone across to his home around midday, and during the early part of the afternoon he decided he would go and get some cash from the nearest cash machine, rather than wait until he&rsquo;d actually got into town. The first cash machine he went to the machine wasn&rsquo;t working, so he then cycled down to another parade of shops, just on a, on a small estate. There were about six or seven shops on this parade, and he put his bicycle against a wall, waited in the queue, and as the lady left the machine and he went to step up, so somebody tried to push in front of him. He naturally, as I believe most people would do, I certainly would do, he said to the person that they should wait their turn. And the person became very angry, went away swearing, and unbeknown to Westley telephoned his brother. During the actual court case that we went through, roughly about 11months after the incident, there was CCTV footage that showed Westley having used the machine and putting his money away, he was talking to the man that had been behind, and they look across in puzzlement towards the car park area where a car had pulled in at speed, out jumped the driver who joined the earlier man that had tried to push in front of Westley, we later came to realise they were brothers. They walked purposefully at speed towards Westley and the other man, who was still looking puzzled, and one appeared to punch Westley in the neck. He had in fact a knife in his hand, and severed Westley&rsquo;s carotid artery. This actually happened at 3 o&rsquo;clock in the afternoon.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>
<p><strong>And how did you hear about it?</strong></p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div>Well again it&rsquo;s such a small world. Somebody actually saw the incident unfolding that knew us as a family, so the news got to me personally before the police had the opportunity to go through the normal process of sending someone and delivering the information in a sensitive way.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>So did you get the information by telephone?</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>The information came by telephone, at that point. The person, I never really established who it was, other than it was someone who knew Westley and knew us as a family, they were saying, &ldquo;We think Westley&rsquo;s been stabbed.&rdquo;</div></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2264, 114, 'David found his son, Simon, bleeding to death on waste ground behind their house. His son had been stabbed to death after he complained when another young man nearly ran him over.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878660777');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2265, 114, 'David found his son, Simon, bleeding to death on waste ground behind their house. His son had been stabbed to death after he complained when another young man nearly ran him over.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>It was 18<sup>th</sup> September 1992, the boys had gone over to a youth centre, this was about half past ten, they knew they had had to be back by half past ten. My wife and I were just about to go to bed and actually got ready for bed, we were actually in bed, knowing the boys would be home any moment, then we received a phone call from one of my sons, saying that the eldest son was being stabbed and beaten up, on waste ground, at the back of our house. We then quickly got dressed and raced over to the waste ground, we met our other son over there, but by the time we got there it was too late, we found our eldest son lying there in a pool of blood.

<p>&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div><strong>How awful.</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>I raced over to him, I tried to get rid of my other son by making him go to ring for an ambulance, my wife was catching up pretty fast, I made her wait because it wasn&rsquo;t the best of sights to see.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>After what seemed like an eternity, the police finally arrived, and at that time the police said, he felt, he thought he could feel the pulse, I couldn&rsquo;t, I tried, I couldn&rsquo;t, I&rsquo;d tried I couldn&rsquo;t feel a pulse on my son, and about five minutes later the ambulance crew came. We were then taken back round our own home, supported by a couple of police officers who were basically with us. And I suppose about half an hour later they announced he was dead.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>Just to go back to that original part, what happened was one of the boys [who killed my son], I should have mentioned it I suppose stole a motor bike and went into the car park where my sons were playing. I think they were kicking a ball around or something, and almost run them over, and what happened at that point was my eldest son who got killed, he went up and kicked the bike and said, &ldquo;What&rsquo;re you doing, you know, you nearly just run us over?&rdquo; And at that point, the boy said, &ldquo;I&rsquo;m going to get my mate to stab you.&rdquo; That&rsquo;s how it all came about. That&rsquo;s how he got the murder charge actually.</div></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<p>People we spoke with speculated about why the tragedy had happened and recalled how they had heard the news.</p>

<p>Some thought that jealously may have played a part, but other reasons were suggested as well.</p>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2266, 114, 'Terri was on holiday when her son Ben was stabbed to death by a young man who had been threatening Ben for a long time because he thought Ben had taken his girlfriend.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878660575');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2267, 114, 'Terri was on holiday when her son Ben was stabbed to death by a young man who had been threatening Ben for a long time because he thought Ben had taken his girlfriend.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>Me and my husband were going away to a wedding. My, my friend, very good friend was getting married in Cyprus. So it was all booked and planned and had been done for about 12 months and the plan was that my mum was going to come to my house, look after the children while myself and my husband went away.</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p>Everything was hunky-dory. Off we went. We arrived in Cyprus late on the 16th June and went down for breakfast the following morning. I changed some currency, sat by the pool and the concierge came looking for me. And I presumed it was something to do with my passport. We&rsquo;d had a bit of a joke because it was about 9 years old my passport and I looked very different. And I was ushered into a room and there was some very official looking people there, so I knew straight away something, it was something quite serious. And they asked me to confirm my name and date of birth and the lady said to me, &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve got some terrible news&rdquo;. And it was a gut feeling, I knew straight away it was Ben. I just said, &ldquo;It&rsquo;s Ben, isn&rsquo;t it?&rdquo; And she said, &ldquo;Yes. I&rsquo;m sorry to tell you but he&rsquo;s been killed.&rdquo; So firstly I thought it was a car crash and the next thing I was just on my knees on, on the floor in this room screaming. I just don&rsquo;t know where it came from. I just started howling. And my husband heard me. He, he was by the pool and I said, &ldquo;What&rsquo;s happened, what&rsquo;s happened?&rdquo; And she said, &ldquo;He&rsquo;s been stabbed. I&rsquo;ve got no other details. We need to get you home.&rdquo; And I said, &ldquo;I need to ring my mother.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p>So they gave me the phone. Unbeknown to me my mother, my mother had also been attacked which I didn&rsquo;t know at the time. I rang my mum&rsquo;s and my brother answered the phone and he said, &ldquo;Look, Terri, just get home.&rdquo; I said, &ldquo;What&rsquo;s happened with mum, what&rsquo;s happened?&rdquo; He said, &ldquo;We&rsquo;ll tell you when you get back.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p>There were very good people at the hotel. They got me, me and my husband into a taxi almost immediately and we were on a plane within about an hour and a half. I drank a litre of brandy believe it or not and I don&rsquo;t&rsquo; know how I walked off that plane. But I did, I just felt so violently sick. So arrived home literally a few hours later, to be told that three people had come into the house late evening, into my family home. They&rsquo;d chased Ben down the road.</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p>He&rsquo;d come home from his job at about eight o&rsquo;clock at night and unbeknown to Ben these three men were waiting for him at the end of our cul-de-sac. They didn&rsquo;t know who he was. One of them did but the other two sort of had a description of him. They chased him down the road. He ran into the front room, just literally opened the front door and ran in and said, &ldquo;Grandma, there&rsquo;s somebody after me.&rdquo; My mum had just got out of the shower, she was still in her dressing gown, and, and my daughters were downstairs. And the next minute there was, there was three men, aged 27, 35 and 17 raining blows on my son.</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p>Just started to batter him. And my mum had no idea what was going on. The next thing my mum was punched and kicked and knocked to the ground and she was, she lost consciousness for a few minutes. When she came round Ben came staggering into the lounge with three stab wounds, one which had pierced his heart. And he, my mum just said, &ldquo;Who, who is it Ben, who&rsquo;s done this?&rdquo; He named that, the man that had done it and the next door neighbour came and tried, tried to resuscitate him. And the ambulance came He were, he was taken to the local hospital but was dead on arrival.</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p>We&rsquo; we&rsquo;ve since found out, obviously during, well basically during the next 24 hours that the younger of the the three, who was seventeen, had had a grudge against Ben for seven months over a girl. The seventeen year old had been out with a girl who&#39;d finished with him in favour of Ben. Ben had started going out with this girl for a couple of months, the relationship had even finished, but he&#39;d been emailing people telling them how much he hated Ben and that he was jealous of him. Ben was the captain of the football team&nbsp;he was,&nbsp;obviously i&#39;m very biased, he was a beautiful looking boy.</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2268, 114, 'Michelle had felt strongly that something dreadful had happened to her mother and was horrified to learn she had been murdered.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878660946');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2269, 114, 'Michelle had felt strongly that something dreadful had happened to her mother and was horrified to learn she had been murdered.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>On the 12<sup>th</sup> October 2005, my Mum had gone to work. My Dad had dropped my Mum off at work and he&rsquo;d called round here at lunchtime, and somebody had telephoned him to say that my Mum hadn&rsquo;t turned up at her job. And instantly that unnerved me, and I felt quite worried. I felt like intuitively I knew something was wrong.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And my Dad said, &ldquo;Well you know your Mum, she&rsquo;s always helping people. She&rsquo;s probably stopped along the way and she&rsquo;s helping someone.&rdquo; And it didn&rsquo;t feel right. It didn&rsquo;t feel right at all. So I said, &ldquo;Well can we ring the hospital?&rdquo; The local hospital, and make sure that nothing&rsquo;s happened. That my Mum&rsquo;s been taken in. So my Dad said, &ldquo;Okay, then we&rsquo;ll do that.&rdquo; And we rang the hospital and no, no, no Mum, no lady of that description or age had been taken in.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>So I got my Dad to keep ringing the place where my Mum was supposed to have gone to work. There was just no answer. Anyway my Dad sat for a while,</div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div>Then he went home. And I came up to bed for a lay down and I started crying because I just knew something terrible had happened. And I kept saying, &ldquo;Oh please Mum where are you? Please be alright.&rdquo; And I couldn&rsquo;t rest. And about, my Dad was due to pick my Mum up at half past three so I was so stressed out and impatient that at half past three I rang my Dad&rsquo;s house, and my Dad answered the phone, and I said, &ldquo;You, you haven&rsquo;t picked Mum up have you?&rdquo; And he said, &ldquo;No, I&rsquo;ve had to ring the police and report your Mum missing.&rdquo; And I thought, &ldquo;No, that doesn&rsquo;t ring true. That doesn&rsquo;t sound right.&rdquo; Because my Dad can&rsquo;t have been there, come home, rung the police, all by half past three, so, something, no somebody&rsquo;s not telling me the truth here.</div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div>So I then had to get up and I felt so anxious and stressed out. And it was a terrible time because I knew something was wrong. And I sat downstairs. I like to watch Home and Away, that&rsquo;s my little treat for the day, and I sat to watch Home and Away, and I was still worried and thinking of my Mum, and then I heard footsteps, footsteps coming up the path, and I stood up and looked out, and I saw my Dad, and I saw a lady and a man with him. And I went to the glass of the door, and I just looked and I just started, I became hysterical, and I started saying, &ldquo;No, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, can&rsquo;t be.&rdquo; And I opened the door. , they came in, and&hellip;</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>They told you the bad news?</strong></div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div>They just said; I was going around the house and wouldn&rsquo;t let anyone near me and I was just saying, &ldquo;Get off me, no, no, no, no, no I can&rsquo;t hear this. No, I can&rsquo;t do this.&rdquo; And my Dad got hold of me and tried to calm me down, and they said, &ldquo;We have found a lady&rsquo;s body, we can&rsquo;t tell you anymore than that.&rdquo; And I was saying, &ldquo;Well what do you mean? Found a lady&rsquo;s body, what can have happened? It is an accident? Has she taken an overdose?&rdquo; And I was running through my mind what could have happened. What has Mum had a fall and cracked her head or? And nobody could tell us anything. And then I just become detached. It was like I came outside of my body and I was looking at myself in this horrendous situation, but my body was trying to protect me. It felt like your body being protective, because it was just too much to take in. It was just way too much to take in. Absolute shock, horror, disbelief, I can&rsquo;t hear this, because it can&rsquo;t be true. And it was horrendous.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>So what happened the next day?</strong><br />
The next day the police came back around, and I remember one of, the man came into the lounge and he said, &ldquo;I&rsquo;m awfully sorry to tell you this, but your wife was actually murdered.&rdquo; And, I remember my Dad&rsquo;s words, he went, &ldquo;Oh God. Oh.&rdquo; And we just couldn&rsquo;t believe it, and like the first thing my Dad said was, &ldquo;Can you tell us, was she sexually assaulted as well?&rdquo; And, and, and he said, &ldquo;No, I can confirm that your wife wasn&rsquo;t assaulted, you know sexually assaulted.&rdquo; And, I don&rsquo;t know, I&rsquo;d, you just go, you just can&rsquo;t take it in, it&rsquo;s just too horrendous, your mind cannot cope with such a huge shock.</div></section></div>
</div>
  </p>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2270, 114, 'Traumatic death caused by strangers', '<p>Lisa found out that her ex-boyfriend, who was homeless at the time of his death, had been stabbed by a man who said he had murdered two people because he was &#39;cleaning up the streets&#39;.</p>

<p>Two of Lisa&#39;s friends were murdered within 7 months.</p>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2271, 114, 'A newspaper article described two people who had been stabbed. Lisa thought one might be her ex-boyfriend so she rang the police and was asked to identify him. The shock and distress made her ill.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878660744');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2272, 114, 'A newspaper article described two people who had been stabbed. Lisa thought one might be her ex-boyfriend so she rang the police and was asked to identify him. The shock and distress made her ill.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>In November 1996, a friend of mine that I used to go out clubbing with, she was murdered in her own home. And we didn&rsquo;t know who had done that for a long time. There was Crimewatch programme on it. And we all had our DNA taken.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>How awful. </strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>It was shocking.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>A shock.</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And then, while we still didn&rsquo;t, still didn&rsquo;t know who had done that, seven months later, my ex-boyfriend was also murdered. And he was murdered in the street.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>How awful. </strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>He was stabbed. And I went and identified his body.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>That must&rsquo;ve been terrible. Did he not have any close relatives living nearby? </strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>He, he&rsquo;s from the north of England and you know living down here and.</div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div>I&rsquo;d read in the paper that day that two people had been stabbed. And I thought that sounds like my ex-boyfriend, I wonder if it is. And I rang the police and they said, &ldquo;Well it possibly is. Can you come and identify him?&rdquo; [laughs]. So I did.</div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div>I went and identified him and it was him.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>Is that first time you&rsquo;ve seen a dead body?</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>No. No. But</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>But to see your boyfriend, it must&rsquo;ve been awful? </strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>It was, yeah. It was shocking.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>Well straight after that there&rsquo;s a gallery round the corner from the coroner and there were some big paintings that I really liked. And it was early in the morning and I went to the gallery. And I went in and sat in this room by myself, surrounded by these beautiful big paintings, Rothko. Do you know Rothko?</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>Beautiful big red&hellip; I just had a peaceful, quiet moment to myself just reflecting. And then, and the after that my life had just changed beyond description. I used to be really outgoing, happy, capable, confident person. And when my first friend died, it was like the world had changed so. And everybody was normal and going on about their business and I was working and I was just thinking, &ldquo;How can you all be normal? People don&rsquo;t&hellip; this doesn&rsquo;t happen in life. I don&rsquo;t get it.&rdquo; And then when my ex was murdered, I was just like, I can&rsquo;t do this anymore, I don&rsquo;t&hellip; I can&rsquo;t. Life doesn&rsquo;t make sense. This is not normal. It&rsquo;s not ok. It&rsquo;s not reality, as people know it. And that I felt like at that point, part of my soul just left. I just thought, &ldquo;I&rsquo;m not staying here, this horrible. I don&rsquo;t like being here.&rdquo; And that I wasn&rsquo;t very well after that.</div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div>I was seriously not very well. And for six and half years, I was very ill. &nbsp;I was classed as disabled.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>All brought on by these murders?</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>Hm. I was suicidal. I was cutting, which I&rsquo;d never considered before.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<p>Adam&rsquo;s brother Lloyd was murdered by &#39;thugs&#39; who hit him over the head with a wooden sign. They beat him unconscious, broke his jaw, and inflicted several other injuries.</p>

<p>Adam said he wanted to cry but felt that he had to be strong for his parents&rsquo; sake, so he choked back his tears. He also had other reactions.</p>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2273, 114, 'When Adam heard news about Lloyd''s death he wanted to cry but almost laughed. He knew laughter was an inappropriate reaction but he felt that it must have been some sort of joke, it was so surreal.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878660681');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2274, 114, 'When Adam heard news about Lloyd''s death he wanted to cry but almost laughed. He knew laughter was an inappropriate reaction but he felt that it must have been some sort of joke, it was so surreal.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>On the 25<sup>th</sup> September 2005 I had a phone call about roughly one o&rsquo;clock in the morning. It was my Dad on the phone and I had to work the next day as well, so I was a bit angry at first because I didn&rsquo;t know why he was phoning me, and I couldn&rsquo;t make out what he was saying, I thought it was interference with the phone signal.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And then as I figured it out, I could hear that he was crying, and he said, &ldquo;Adam, Lloyd&rsquo;s, Lloyd&rsquo;s been attacked, he&rsquo;s dead&rdquo;, and I remember that, well I just went into a daze really, I can&rsquo;t even, I can&rsquo;t even say I felt anything, I didn&rsquo;t even feel sad, I didn&rsquo;t I didn&rsquo;t know what to think, I was just like a zombie after that. And they said that the police were going to come and pick me up and take me to the, to the hospital, so that I could meet them there and see him and things like that. I got dressed, I was wandering around the house just aimlessly, I mean my housemate was still up and he said you know, &ldquo;What&rsquo;s the matter?&rdquo; And I said, &ldquo;My brother&rsquo;s been, my brother&rsquo;s dead.&rdquo; And I just didn&rsquo;t, I didn&rsquo;t have any sort of reaction on my face at all. Although to be honest and I don&rsquo;t like saying it, because I think it&rsquo;s a really weird thing, to react like but, I might as well be honest because other people might have reacted the same to news like that, I actually laughed, and I can never understand why, but then it happened again, I was with some friends and we were having a joke and a laugh just trying to lighten the mood, and I can remember we were laughing so hard our sides were splitting and I can remember because I was laughing so much it just suddenly turned into this horrendous amount of crying, but I managed to stop myself before it got like that, because there was this thing at the back of my mind the whole time saying, &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t cry, don&rsquo;t be stupid,&rdquo; you know, &ldquo;Pull yourself together.&rdquo; So, even now if I start crying, two sniffles later and then I stop because I feel stupid even when I&rsquo;m on my own.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>Why do you think you feel stupid?</strong></div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div>I don&rsquo;t know, it&rsquo;s one of the things that was said to me by a member of the family, I don&rsquo;t know whether, I don&rsquo;t know whether it was that but I was told, &ldquo;Be strong for your parents&rdquo;, so every time I started to get upset I would choke it back, and walk away, anything to not let myself be seen crying and things like that. And I think as a result of that that maybe one of the reasons that I&rsquo;m unable to cry as much now because I&rsquo;ve got this built in thing saying, &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t, don&rsquo;t cry, don&rsquo;t be silly, be strong.&rdquo; and going to back to before going to the hospital, my parents turned up in the end, and they said the police were unable to pick me up and take me to the hospital because they didn&rsquo;t have enough police cars. They didn&rsquo;t have enough resources. So my parents turned up.</div></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<p>Jayne&rsquo;s husband Jonathan and Dolores&rsquo; son Tom were both killed by strangers who were known&nbsp;to be mental ill at the time.</p>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2275, 114, 'Jayne''s husband, Jonathan, was waiting for an underground train. He was stabbed by a man who was mentally ill, apparently an indiscriminate attack.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878660463');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2276, 114, 'Jayne''s husband, Jonathan, was waiting for an underground train. He was stabbed by a man who was mentally ill, apparently an indiscriminate attack.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>Well, it was it was a Christmas in 1992, and I&rsquo;d recently got married to Jonathan, my husband, we got married in the September, after a very short courtship and Jonathan had moved to London and he&rsquo;d only been in London for about six weeks, and he was just settling down and we were settling into a new routine of life, and his family had come over from Italy for Christmas, and he had gone to the airport to meet them. There was his mother and his father and his two brothers and his sister, and he&rsquo;d gone to the airport to meet everybody from the flight from Italy, to bring them back across London on the flat, and there wasn&rsquo;t enough room in one of my friends cars for a lift, so Jonathan had to travel on the underground to come, to come home really, and he came home with his brother on the underground. So they had to travel across London on the Victoria line up to Finsbury Park, and at Finsbury Park, when they got to Finsbury Park there was a man on the platform who went up behind Jon and attacked Jon and stabbed him three times in the face and one of the blows pierced Jon&rsquo;s artery above his right eye, and he didn&rsquo;t regain consciousness, so he didn&rsquo;t have a chance after the attack really. And the man who killed Jon, who attacked Jon that day put the knife back in his pocket, the tube driver was arriving into the platform when they saw the attack happening and stopped the tube, and the man put the knife back in his pocket, sat on the train and waited to be arrested.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And a policeman came to my flat where I was waiting for the whole family to come back, to tell me that; I answered the door to the police and they told me that Jon had been involved in an incident, and that I needed to go to the hospital. And I realised perhaps that I needed to ask somebody to be there with me, so I rang a friend because my family lived in Cornwall, and went to the hospital, and on the way to the hospital I was informed that Jon had been stabbed in the face and I asked the police officer whether they knew that, whether he was going to die or not, and he said that he didn&rsquo;t, that he didn&rsquo;t know. And I remember getting to the hospital and there being lots of police there, yes, men in suits I call them, and Jon&rsquo;s brother, and they made us wait, the clinical team made us wait because they said they were giving Jon a brain scan. That there was a good sign because Jon was still breathing, so there was that, you know, that was a good sign, and they, we waited.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>I was with Jon&rsquo;s brother and Jon&rsquo;s brother, I remember him walking up to me and saying, &ldquo;He just came from nowhere,&rdquo; because the attack was completely indiscriminate. I mean the gentleman who killed Jon literally had been very menacing on the platform and other people had seen him and moved away, but Jon had not seen the gentleman, and Jon&rsquo;s brother had seen him on the platform that he was behind them and motioned for Jon to move, and as Jon was moving he stabbed him from behind, so Jon and, all the evidence suggests that they didn&rsquo;t even make eye contact, so the attack was extremely indiscriminate.</div></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2277, 114, 'Tom was stabbed 22 times by a young man with schizophrenia. Dolores describes how she heard the terrible news that her son had been killed.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878660806');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2278, 114, 'Tom was stabbed 22 times by a young man with schizophrenia. Dolores describes how she heard the terrible news that her son had been killed.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>It was 15<sup>th</sup> of September 2006 and we were home asleep, myself and my partner. And we&rsquo;d just come back from holiday in Switzerland. And we&rsquo;re fast asleep. It was as far as I&rsquo;ve been told, about eleven o&rsquo;clock at night or just after. And the phone rang and I thought, &nbsp;and I always knew that whenever the phone rings at that time, there&rsquo;s something wrong. But I always get annoyed that someone can ring us at this time and usually it&rsquo;s my brother or my sister. They both live abroad. Or someone I know that lives abroad and I reached for the phone and then answered. And someone&rsquo;s voice&hellip; and I can&rsquo;t remember all words. But all I remember is that they said, &rdquo;Fatally wounded&rdquo; [crying].</div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div>And I think &hellip; vaguely remember him asking if I am Tom Easton&rsquo;s mother. And when he said fatally wounded I, I didn&rsquo;t comprehend. I just asked, &ldquo;Where is he, which hospital?&rdquo;</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>&nbsp;&ldquo;What&rsquo;s happened?&rdquo; And he said, &ldquo;I&rsquo;m sorry, he was fatally wounded.&rdquo; And then I just &hellip; I couldn&rsquo;t speak anymore and I passed the phone to my partner.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And I just I just went and sat on the floor by the bed. And screaming, that&rsquo;s all I remember then [crying].</div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div>Then I ran downstairs because I didn&rsquo;t want to listen to what he talking to the policeman about. I just ran downstairs and, and, and grabbed the phone and I didn&rsquo;t know which number &hellip; what I was trying to do whether I was trying to ring someone or tell someone.</div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div>And I&rsquo;ve been told after that I rang a friend. And she wasn&rsquo;t there and &nbsp;or she was and probably asleep. And I was just screaming, &ldquo;Tom was killed&rdquo; [crying].</div></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<p>Some people we talked to said that the people who had committed the crimes had been a family member of or were a friend of the victim.</p>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2279, 114, 'Marcus'' fiancée, Louise, was stabbed by a man who pretended to offer Marcus work. Initially Marcus was treated as a suspect. He was shocked when the police told him Louise was dead.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878660875');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2280, 114, 'Marcus'' fiancée, Louise, was stabbed by a man who pretended to offer Marcus work. Initially Marcus was treated as a suspect. He was shocked when the police told him Louise was dead.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>Yes. Just twenty-two years ago my fianc&eacute;e was brutally stabbed to death in south London. And the way that came about I actually met a man who offered me a gainful employment, at the time I was unemployment. And we decided to proceed with me taking the job he gave me. And over the next two to three days we became, I guess friends. Little did I know that the time that the person I&rsquo;d met had only recently been released from a jail in Scotland, for cutting somebody&rsquo;s throat. Having served his time, having been released, he&rsquo;d only been out of jail six weeks. And he came to London with a view; so I&rsquo;m told by the police etcetera, that he came to London with a view to kill somebody to go back to jail. I met him in a bar. We became friends. And in the next three days, we decided to invite him to our home for dinner. And on that particular evening, he asked me to make a phone call. In those days, it was 1987&hellip;mobile phones were built like a brick. And nobody had any because you couldn&rsquo;t afford them.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And also at the time we&rsquo;d just moved to our new house and we didn&rsquo;t, we weren&rsquo;t on the phone. So I had to go to use a red phone box, which I did. I didn&rsquo;t realise at the time, he gave me a false name and false number to ring. And it was that point in, in, in the evening when I tried ringing the person he gave me the phone number for, I realised something was drastically wrong. And by the time I reached the end of my street, I, all I could see was police lights and cars trained on what I thought was my neighbour&rsquo;s house. In actual fact it was mine. And as I ran jumping fences and fences because I didn&rsquo;t know what was going on, I was panicking. And the nearer and nearer I got, I realised that it was my, my house. And I rapidly approached the door and was knocked back the police and pinned to the ground.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>How awful.</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And at that point I was screaming, I didn&rsquo;t know what had gone on. And all the neighbours were out in the streets. And I was taken into the back of a police car and arrested at that point. It, it was a matter of minutes.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And it was during&hellip; on the, the enormous journey back from the property in south London to the nearest police station, we were doing over a hundred miles an hour. And on the radio, the police radio, they said, &ldquo;Slow down it&rsquo;s ok, we&rsquo;ve, we&rsquo;ve got the man&rdquo;. There was a lot more stronger words were said than that.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>But they said, &ldquo;Slow down we&rsquo;ve already got him but we&rsquo;ve got to keep Marcus in custody&rdquo;.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>So you were a suspect to start with?</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>Yes I was charged.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>But it wasn&rsquo;t until we got back to the police station, about an hour and half later, that &hellip; I was stripped and given a paper suit. I still&hellip; nobody&rsquo;s&hellip;nobody told me why. And I was asking what had happened all the time. And they said, &ldquo;We can&rsquo;t tell you&rdquo;. And eventually they got me into a room, out of the cell and they said, &ldquo;We got some bad news for you, your wife&rsquo;s dead&rdquo;. And I said, &ldquo;Well that&rsquo;s ok because I&rsquo;m not married&rdquo;. But we both wore wedding rings because we couldn&rsquo;t afford to get married. So we both wore wedding rings and pretended. And he said, he gave my fianc&eacute;&rsquo;s name and he just said, &ldquo;She died an hour ago&rdquo;.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And I said, &ldquo;She can&rsquo;t be dead because I only left her an hour ago&rdquo;.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>So I was in total shock. And then I was sedated by the police surgeon. And I woke up the next morning thinking it was a bad dream.</div></section></div>
</div>
  </p>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2281, 114, 'Traumatic death caused by family or friends', '<p>For example, Alison&#39;s estranged husband killed two of their children after they had had a violent argument.</p>

<p>Some of the killers were ex-partners of or were in close relationships with the people who were victims.</p>

<p>Angela talked about her cousin, who had been murdered by her boyfriend while living in Jamaica. She also talked about a friend in the UK whose grandson had stabbed her to death.</p>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tText v4x3 hideit''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2282, 114, 'Angela had recently lost a cousin and a friend through homicide. These two deaths devastated her. She heard about her friend''s death over the telephone.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''textOnly''><div>Text only<div><img src=''/img/book.png''/></div><span>Read below</span></div></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2283, 114, 'Angela had recently lost a cousin and a friend through homicide. These two deaths devastated her. She heard about her friend''s death over the telephone.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>HIDE TEXT</div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>Yes it was in January of this year. I&rsquo;m not sure exactly what happened but as I&rsquo;ve been told her boyfriend had attacked her [Angela&rsquo;s cousin] with a machete and she was found, because she was found because at that time she was looking after a little girl, and the little girl was in the house crying and the neighbours noticed that this girl had been crying for a number of hours, if not even a day or so, and they went to investigate and they found my cousin on the on the floor, with a massive head injury, the coroner said had she received help a lot earlier, she would&rsquo;ve survived, but she basically bled to death leaving three children, the youngest of whom is 15. Well it&rsquo;s still traumatic.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>You said somebody else, a friend of yours died? </strong></div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div>A friend of mine,</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>Was that in this country?</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>Yes, a friend of mine that I&rsquo;d met about five and a half years ago now, six years ago, I met this woman. She had lost her son, which, who had been murdered, and because of the nature of the work that we do she was a keen, anti-gun and anti-violence campaigner and that&rsquo;s how we met. And she had such a warming personality that I don&rsquo;t think I&rsquo;ve come across anybody that has or will say something negative about this individual.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And I was at work, in June, and I received a call from a friend, and I couldn&rsquo;t even understand what my friend was saying, she was so distraught. So that, immediately kind of like you know panicked me a little bit, and like, &ldquo;What, what, what, what, what.&rdquo; She couldn&rsquo;t get her words out so I decided to wait until she could, so instead of saying &ldquo;What, what, what&rdquo;, because it just didn&rsquo;t make any sense, I waited and after a while she just said that our friend had been murdered.</div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div><strong>What a terrible shock.</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>She&rsquo;d been found stabbed and she had been murdered by her grandson.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>Her grandson?</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And it just didn&rsquo;t ring, it just didn&rsquo;t ring true, I don&rsquo;t know. And I was in this little office at work because I take my private calls somewhere else, and before I could even realise it I was just wailing.</div></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tText v4x3 hideit''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2284, 114, 'Carole''s son was stabbed by his ex-girlfriend, who was jealous of a new relationship. A police officer told Carole the terrible news at her work.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''textOnly''><div>Text only<div><img src=''/img/book.png''/></div><span>Read below</span></div></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2285, 114, 'Carole''s son was stabbed by his ex-girlfriend, who was jealous of a new relationship. A police officer told Carole the terrible news at her work.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>HIDE TEXT</div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>I was working on November 30<sup>th</sup> and my line manager came and asked me to go into the office, there was a police officer wanting to speak to me and a WPC. I just felt sick, I said, &ldquo;What has happened?&rdquo; and in my mind I knew it was my son. I went into the office and it was a PC who I knew who was known to our family that, as a friend. And he just said, &ldquo;I hate to break this news to you,&rdquo; and he told us that our, well our son had been killed. Our son was at the other end of the country, he was in the forces, but the incident had nothing at all to do with the forces. I heard he&rsquo;d been stabbed by a female and I knew straight away who it would be. It was an ex girl friend who was very jealous, who would not let him go, our son was at his happiest, he had a new girlfriend and everything was going very well for him, but he&rsquo;d almost been stalked by this person. I knew my husband would be collecting me from work within about the next half hour so I explained this to the police. I just wanted to be in another world.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>The police went outside to meet my husband and I was afraid what was going to happen when we told him. Anyway we told my husband and the reaction was as I anticipated. I rang my daughter from the office that I was in. How I was able to do that I don&rsquo;t know but I just rang her and asked if she could go home, we just wanted to see her at home.</div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div><strong>What was your husband&rsquo;s reaction? You said it was as you anticipated.</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>He just went white, his head in his hands, and I think we stayed strong for each other, and knowing that we had our daughter. I think if it had just been me on my own I would have just, just collapsed. And legs just went to jelly, it was just surreal.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>Awful.</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And we got home and our daughter came home and she knew straight away when she saw the police also. She did know the police officer, and my reaction then was how was I going to tell my father? My father was 88 years of age. Very, very close to our son. Our son had been at war. He&rsquo;d written to him religiously as our son had written to him. And it was odd really because our son had spent a couple of hours with his granddad two days prior to this incident.</div></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tAudio v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2286, 114, 'Julie''s sister, Shirley, was stabbed to death in her own home by an ex-boyfriend, who had beaten her up on other occasions. The police told Julie what had happened.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/1055213123');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2287, 114, 'Julie''s sister, Shirley, was stabbed to death in her own home by an ex-boyfriend, who had beaten her up on other occasions. The police told Julie what had happened.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>My sister, she was murdered by her ex-partner, two months after her birthday. He beat her up on her birthday, fracturing her cheek in two places and was sent to prison twice and kept getting let out on bail and kept harassing her. And then the last time he got let out on bail, we don&rsquo;t know whether, it, he says my sister let him back in the house but my nephew said that he didn&rsquo;t, he didn&rsquo;t know he were there. And he stabbed her in her carotid artery and she bled to death.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>Which year was this?</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>It was the 11<sup>th</sup> September 2006.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>It happened at tea, round tea-time-ish but they didn&rsquo;t get in touch with anybody because obviously they didn&rsquo;t know any details or anything. They came to the house, the police, and my husband and myself was out and my son kept ringing me, so when I finally answered the phone, I said to him, &ldquo;What do you want?&rdquo; He said, &ldquo;The police are here. They&rsquo;ve been twice.&rdquo; &ldquo;What have you done?&rdquo; And he were like, &ldquo;We haven&rsquo;t done nothing mum. It&rsquo;s you they want to see.&rdquo;</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And we were sat in the car, the car and my husband and myself were saying what kind of act, you know, &ldquo;I wonder what the police want.&rdquo; And I said, &ldquo;Oh it might be my mum&rdquo; because we&rsquo;d just dropped her off in Birmingham to her, to her friend&rsquo;s the night before. And my husband said, &ldquo;It&rsquo;s Shirley.&rdquo; He just knew it was my sister even before anybody told us. He just knew it was her. And when we got home he sent the children out. They were all obviously, they were concerned they wanted to know why the police was here.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>Yes, of course. </strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And the officer sent them back out and he told us. And because I was crying and screaming, the children come running in and they wanted know what was up.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>So then they was all crying then and it was quite difficult. And then my next concern was my sister&rsquo;s son. So where is he?</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>You know what&rsquo;s &hellip; well first of all I wanted to know what had happened to her. And he just said, &ldquo;I can&rsquo;t divulge that but we think she&rsquo;s been murdered.&rdquo;&nbsp;</div></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tAudio v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2288, 114, 'Linda''s son, Kevin, was stabbed to death by his sister''s ex-boyfriend. Linda heard what had had happened when Kevin''s work mate phoned her almost immediately after the murder took place.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/1055200786');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2289, 114, 'Linda''s son, Kevin, was stabbed to death by his sister''s ex-boyfriend. Linda heard what had had happened when Kevin''s work mate phoned her almost immediately after the murder took place.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div>Yes it was September 2007, my eldest son, who was 27 was at his workplace, there&rsquo;d been a disagreement with my youngest daughter and her boyfriend that she hadn&rsquo;t been with for a couple of weeks. She wanted some stuff out of his house. He was being very rude and wasn&rsquo;t going to let her have it so she did what I think most young sisters do, she called on her brother for help, with absolutely disastrous effects because basically the ex-boyfriend went to my son&rsquo;s place of work all ready with a knife in his pocket that was ready to use. In my eyes he had full intention to do what he did and he went there and that&rsquo;s exactly what he did. And a small disagreement ensued. My son was trying to tell him to just leave my daughter alone and give her a break. You know, if it was the constant texting and phone calls that were going on that he&rsquo;d get her a new sim card...</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>&hellip;and then that would be the end of it. &nbsp;But the person just lunged at him basically, stabbed him straight through the heart.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>How awful.</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>He didn&rsquo;t realise himself that he&rsquo;d been stabbed at first. He was running round at work but within minutes he&rsquo;d collapsed.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And an ambulance was called and he taken to our local general hospital. He put up a terrific fight. They resuscitated him ten times, but he was a very fit person.</div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div>But not meant to be. And he died five hours later.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>I&rsquo;m so sorry.</strong></div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>So&hellip;</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><strong>How did you first hear about it?</strong></div>

<div><strong>&nbsp;</strong></div>

<div>I&rsquo;d heard first thing in the morning. Funnily enough, when my daughter had come in and she&rsquo;d, she&rsquo;d run to me on a Saturday and said, &ldquo;Mum, you know, my ex is on his way to Kevin&rsquo;s workplace&rdquo;. I then rang him and he said he was fine and not to worry and nothing was going to happen and that if he did turn up he would talk him out of it&hellip;</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>&hellip;and, you know, try and soothe things out and &nbsp;that&rsquo;s where it went from there. So I rang him. He said everything was okay. I think I&rsquo;ve just repeated that, haven&rsquo;t I? And put the phone down. And then literally two minutes later the mobile rang again and Kevin&rsquo;s name come up and I thought everything was fine and it was his workmate and he&rsquo;d said that Kevin had in those minutes just been stabbed.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And I rushed down there with my daughter, it took us about, because it was a Saturday morning, it took us a good 15, 20 minutes to get down there. The ambulances were there. Kevin was already in the ambulance. They wouldn&rsquo;t let us in. They didn&rsquo;t give us any information. All we could do was follow the ambulance to the hospital, which we did. I super-glued my bumper to their bumper &hellip;</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>&hellip;so that I didn&rsquo;t miss them through any lights or roundabouts and made our way to the hospital where we had to wait in the family room &nbsp;while we were given, you know, various progress points that started off, not very good odds. And the odds didn&rsquo;t change. And went worse and worse and worse until eventually he couldn&rsquo;t be saved and he died.</div></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<p>Last reviewed: June 2025<br />
Last updated: October 2015.</p></div><p>Copyright &copy; 2024 University of Oxford. All rights reserved.</p>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO articles (id, subcategory_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (115, 115, 'Coming to terms with a terminal prognosis', '<h1>Caring for someone with a terminal illness</h1>
      <h2>Coming to terms with a terminal prognosis</h2><div class=''content''><div>Waiting for a diagnosis can be very stressful when you know someone is unwell but you don&rsquo;t know why. Some people had to wait a long time for a diagnosis; others were told a suspected diagnosis while further tests were done before the diagnosis was confirmed. Sometimes doctors&rsquo; initial suspicions were wrong, meaning that people were given one or more inaccurate diagnoses before the correct one. Jane was initially relieved that George was not diagnosed with Motor Neurone Disease although later tests showed he did have it. Saba sought a second opinion about her mother&rsquo;s symptoms, and Mary moved her husband to a private hospital to speed up the diagnostic process. A speech therapist friend suspected Dick&rsquo;s wife had MND and told her that she should see her doctor. The carer sometimes realised what was wrong or found out the diagnosis before the patient had been formally told. For instance Una had researched her husband&rsquo;s symptoms on the internet and sneaked a look at his notes on the consultant&rsquo;s desk to confirm her suspicions.</div>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878672489');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2290, 115, 'Because of her experience as a medical secretary, Maggie recognised that Donald''s GP was using a cancer referral form; later the family agreed that he should be told that the doctor suspected cancer.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878672489');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2291, 115, 'Because of her experience as a medical secretary, Maggie recognised that Donald''s GP was using a cancer referral form; later the family agreed that he should be told that the doctor suspected cancer.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>So the following week he was supposed to see the GP anyway and that morning he was going to work at the bowls club so he drove down and I drove down too. I walked down to meet him afterwards and I got in the car with him and, my husband was the sort of person who could drive anything anywhere. We got in the car. We came out of the bowl&rsquo;s club, bowl&rsquo;s club car park and he turned the wrong way, which really threw him. Going round a roundabout, to sort himself out, he nearly hit the gate post. We, eventually, we got to the, we were near the complex where the doctors is and we missed that turning so he turned into the station car park, once again, narrowly missing the entrance. We came back along the road to the mini-roundabout and if I hadn&rsquo;t shouted he would have hit the person on the left hand side, who fair enough, should have slowed down, but wasn&rsquo;t giving way. When we got into the car park for the doctors, which is part of the shopping centre next to Halfords there were no spaces so he parked on the zig zags, where which is used for the ambulances and fire engines and I said, &ldquo;Darling, you can&rsquo;t park there.&rdquo; I said, &ldquo;I tell you what, go in a disabled space and I&rsquo;ll keep an eye out.&rdquo; So he pulled into the middle of two disabled spaces and he parked diagonally across them and couldn&rsquo;t see anything wrong with the way he&rsquo;d parked.<br />
<br />
So went into the doctors and he spoke to her and I explained what had happened and he explained that he was, he didn&rsquo;t have headaches but he had times when his head didn&rsquo;t feel right and he just, he just basically didn&rsquo;t feel himself. And he and I think he was having trouble with distances and things like that.<br />
<br />
Anyway, she said that she would refer him to a neurologist. I asked her for a copy of the letter and she said, &ldquo;No. She was going to do a form. Well, I knew immediately it was the local, it was the cancer form for the local network so I asked her this, using the initials so that Donald wouldn&rsquo;t twig, and she agreed that&rsquo;s what it was.<br />
<br />
So she, we came home. He immediately fell asleep on the couch in the conservatory and slept for a couple of hours and, in the meantime, I rang my stepdaughter to say, &ldquo;Look. I think this, think he may have brain tumour.&rdquo; And I said, &ldquo;I think perhaps I need to tell him. What do you think?&rdquo; So we agreed that Donald always liked to know what was going on so when he woke up, I explained, as gently as possible, that this is probably what they were going to test him for and he was all right.<br />
&nbsp;</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2292, 115, 'John felt he had to wait too long for the diagnosis of his son''s Leukaemia.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878672629');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2293, 115, 'John felt he had to wait too long for the diagnosis of his son''s Leukaemia.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>And then, eventually, he got transferred to a regional hospital, where he was treated, and that was the first time, and this so this is this is three and a half weeks after I brought him home and almost a week since he&rsquo;d been in hospital, we eventually, get told that he&rsquo;s got leukaemia. <br />
<br />
So we, we had that horrible time right at the start. We knew something was wrong. It was obviously serious. He was getting worse. He was very weak by the end, he could hardly stand up on his own, you know, as a as a very fit and active twenty year old. And, and people were kind of skirting around it but they wouldn&rsquo;t actually say anything, because they didn&rsquo;t know for certain. And, and so, you know, that wasn&rsquo;t that wasn&rsquo;t good and I think certainly, from my point of view, this is because cancer is so rare in young people, it, it takes far too long to diagnose it really and we experienced that. <br />
&nbsp;</p></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<div>Being told the condition was terminal was usually a harrowing experience and carers felt a wide range of emotions including fear, distress, anger and sadness. Some found the diagnosis was a complete shock that came &lsquo;out of the blue&rsquo;.</div>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2294, 115, 'Ruth was shocked when her mother was diagnosed with terminal cancer; although she didn''t have a good relationship with her mother Ruth felt the need to see her every day while she was ill.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878672718');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2295, 115, 'Ruth was shocked when her mother was diagnosed with terminal cancer; although she didn''t have a good relationship with her mother Ruth felt the need to see her every day while she was ill.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>Because I had an iffy relationship with my mum, not very close and when she was eighty she had this birthday party, first one she&rsquo;s ever had. And a week later went into hospital and when she came out, they hadn&rsquo;t done the operation and I was with her and the Macmillan, not the Macmillan unit. I was with her at the hospital when they told her that she would die of it. It was terminal cancer. I was as shocked as my mum. Well I say that, I probably wasn&rsquo;t as shocked as my mum.<br />
<br />
But she was very shaken, very quiet and I thought, &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t want to deal with this.&rdquo; Anyway, we went to my sister&rsquo;s and I had to tell my sister, which was the hardest thing because we&rsquo;re both crying, but it was shock more than anything because neither of us were that close to her, although she was an okay mum but circumstances.<br />
<br />
So but we thought it was in the future so we all got used to it and my mum lived alone and has lots of friends. She&rsquo;s quite social. She had lots of friends and she seemed all okay with it. She was pretty stoic, but I felt the need to see her every day and my sister didn&rsquo;t, as a matter of, my sister&rsquo;s got a disability. So I went to see her, called in there every day and it wasn&rsquo;t a chore because she&rsquo;s quite a positive person but, obviously, she was brave and, except in the night time when she was on her own.<br />
&nbsp;</p></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<div>For others the diagnosis was expected and explained the symptoms. Where the patient was elderly a terminal prognosis was easier to accept. Theadora said she took her cue from her sick mother and remained calm. The diagnosis also brought a sense of relief to carers who had been frustrated by the lack of a definite diagnosis or who had been searching the internet to find out what was wrong. Most carers received the news during a medical consultation; a nurse was sometimes present. David (Interview 35) said, &ldquo;I think it was delivered in quite the right way; it was fairly straight forward really&rdquo;. Not everyone was happy with the way the diagnosis was delivered. While some consultants were very direct and matter of fact, others used unclear language or left carers and patients unsure of the implications of the diagnosis. English was not Saba&rsquo;s mother&rsquo;s first language, which may explain perceived difficulties in how best to deliver the diagnosis to her.</div>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2296, 115, 'Saba was angry because she felt the consultant had expected her to break the news to her mother, then when they did tell her themselves they did it in a ''very cool way''.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878673059');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2297, 115, 'Saba was angry because she felt the consultant had expected her to break the news to her mother, then when they did tell her themselves they did it in a ''very cool way''.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>And they rang me and sort of said, &ldquo;This is the situation, it has been diagnosed as cancer, as we had feared, metastases, we need to obviously let you know&rdquo;. And I said, &ldquo;Well what are you saying, do I, are you going to tell my mother?&rdquo; They said, &ldquo;Well it&rsquo;s up to you.&rdquo; But I felt that they were pushing the ball in my court, which I thought was quite unprofessional because I just thought it&rsquo;s about my mother and it&rsquo;s about you. I&rsquo;m her daughter but it&rsquo;s not for me to... how do I break the news to Mother? I can&rsquo;t tell her these things, you know, I&rsquo;m not professionally geared up.<br />
<br />
So I literally had to sort of say to them, &ldquo;What are asking me to do here? Are you at any stage going to call my mother in, I&rsquo;m expecting you to break the news to her. All I can do is be there to support her and I don&rsquo;t feel&hellip; I know nothing about the situation that I can even break the news to her before we come to see you&rdquo;. They just said to me, &ldquo;Well it&rsquo;s up to you how you want to deal with it&rdquo;. So anyway I said, &ldquo;I think it&rsquo;s for you to tell her because there&rsquo;ll be questions that she may ask&rdquo;.<br />
<br />
So I took&hellip; we went to see the consultant with my mum. And they just, just sort of said to my mother, &ldquo;We&rsquo;ve had the results back from your cancer&hellip; I&rsquo;m afraid, you know, you have cancer, and it will be passed on in the next two weeks&hellip; we&rsquo;re passing it on to an oncologist and we&rsquo;re going to ask you to just go&hellip; we need you to go and do a blood test now and we&rsquo;ll get back to you&rdquo;. And I was shocked at, you know, I thought they would have somebody there, you know, somebody, a Macmillan nurse or somebody there when they told her the news to sort of help to sort of counsel her at this stage. But my mother I think was just shocked because she just went and said, &ldquo;Oh okay&rdquo;. Obviously she, there&rsquo;s a language barrier. She could speak English but I don&rsquo;t she felt confident when it comes to an emotional matter.<br />
<br />
And, you know, I didn&rsquo;t really know what to say to her. What do you say? So I, I was really angry with the consultant at how they&rsquo;d handled it, that it was done in a very sort of cool way. And I can understand from their perspective, on a professional level that&rsquo;s the only way they could deal with it. But here you&rsquo;re talking about, you know, a disease, that affects not just you on physical level, emotional level and all the sort of people around you, and it was a great unknown. At no point did they tell her that her cancer was terminal. At no point did they sort of say, &ldquo;This is the situation&rdquo;. She didn&rsquo;t ask any questions because she didn&rsquo;t know what to ask at that stage. And I think she was just absorbing the shock, the shock of it.</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2298, 115, 'Simon had mixed feelings about the way the consultant delivered his wife''s terminal diagnosis but was grateful that she showed humanity.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878673092');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2299, 115, 'Simon had mixed feelings about the way the consultant delivered his wife''s terminal diagnosis but was grateful that she showed humanity.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><div><b>How was that? How was that consultation managed?</b></div>

<div><b>&nbsp;</b></div>

<div>Well it was, looking back it, it was, it was managed probably as well as it can be. Because I understand about, more now about what they have to do, they cannot pull any punches I think, I mean you know so I understand now but at the time it feels like it&rsquo;s managed badly because, well I&rsquo;m still very mixed. I think that if someone is going to die, and that&rsquo;s a foregone conclusion and the consultant knows that they are going to die, and there&rsquo;s nothing you can do about that, in a way I don&rsquo;t see any harm in pulling the punch slightly to start with. You know on the basis that if you, if you give the news at least gradually to an extent you just soften the blow. I mean in a way I don&rsquo;t see the harm in that.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><b>What did the consultant say then?</b></div>

<div><b>&nbsp;</b></div>

<div>She said that, you know you go into a little clinical room, on, off a corridor, and sit down and she took a nurse with her. She in fact took two nurses with her this time, you know so by this stage I know that if someone takes a nurse with them it&rsquo;s serious.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And she said, very matter of factly, she said, &ldquo;Well you know,&rdquo; along the lines of, you, it is cancer and it is malignant, and she said, it&rsquo;s in your pancreas and we can&rsquo;t operate. I think fairly early on she said, &ldquo;We can&rsquo;t remove it.&rdquo; And then you know so you know, I then said, &ldquo;Well, okay so you can&rsquo;t remove it, but,&rdquo; and she said, &ldquo;Well we can give you chemotherapy to contain it.&rdquo; So I then said, &ldquo;Okay well, so you can contain it then indefinitely?&rdquo; Because it, you know, you just, I think you go into shock and you clutch at straws, and she said, &ldquo;There is only so long that you can contain it for you know, it will develop but we, but we can&rsquo;t get rid of it.&rdquo;</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And I think I then was carrying on with, &ldquo;Okay, well we&rsquo;ll contain it then.&rdquo; So she, I think she knew then that the message wasn&rsquo;t getting through. But the way the, then she just, she then just said, I think it was along the lines of, you know, &ldquo;You&rsquo;re looking at, you know, another two years, and basically you need,&rdquo; she said, &ldquo;Basically you need to make provision for your children now.&rdquo;</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div><b>So that was quite shocking.</b></div>

<div><b>&nbsp;</b></div>

<div>And that, it was that phrase, just stuck with me all, you know even, even now I just think, it was sort of too, she wanted, she was using a shock factor to make the point. And I was, I&rsquo;m very mixed, I&rsquo;m very mixed because you know she would&rsquo;ve known that we&rsquo;re, that as soon as she told us the news you go into shock. I mean you go into a deep shock where you&rsquo;re not really thinking straight. And maybe they&rsquo;re trained to, you know maybe, I don&rsquo;t know, but then you, maybe they use shock to sort of shock you out of it. You know the equivalent of slapping someone&rsquo;s face. Where she you know, and she knew that mentioning the children would make it real.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>The other thing I do remember is that she actually sort of almost welled up at one point. And I was so glad actually, that obviously that sounds horrible, I don&rsquo;t mean that I&rsquo;m glad that she was upset, it&rsquo;s just that that level of humanity you need because it&rsquo;s so clinical.</div>

<div>&nbsp;</div>

<div>And they&rsquo;re so well practised in delivering the news, that when she sort of slightly welled up because she&rsquo;s, she had young children, in fact she was pregnant when she gave us the news so she must&rsquo;ve felt for Karen. They were probably of similar age so she must have felt for Karen qui</div></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2300, 115, 'David had not realised how serious his wife''s illness was before the diagnosis. The doctor told them the news in a direct way but used terms he didn''t understand.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878673062');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2301, 115, 'David had not realised how serious his wife''s illness was before the diagnosis. The doctor told them the news in a direct way but used terms he didn''t understand.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>I think probably Fiona was a bit stronger that I was at that point. I found that very difficult. I don&rsquo;t know if that&rsquo;s because perhaps she had a, a bit more of an inkling that there was something fairly major going on inside. And whether or not I felt, well I just didn&rsquo;t realise how major that might have been. I found that difficult. But, I remember at the time being very upset.<br />
<br />
<strong>I&rsquo;m sure. How was the news delivered?</strong></p>

<p><br />
Fairly matter of fact. The consultant for her liver, was the person who passed the information to us. And I think it was delivered in, in quite the right way. It was fairly straight forward really. We went to a room to discuss what the findings were. And didn&rsquo;t beat around the bush, went fairly quickly to what it was. Mentioned words, which needed clarification like a growth, you&rsquo;re not quite sure what that means. And then palliative care came up and that was a word I didn&rsquo;t understand.<br />
<br />
<strong>When you said he explained what the growth meant, what did he say?</strong><br />
<br />
I&hellip; well I think at that point they hadn&rsquo;t actually diagnosed what it was in terms of a cancer. But I think cancer was what he was telling us. And but&hellip; the precise type of cancer source and all of those things, they still needed to do tests on. But in a very short time, we were talking to the doctor, we were put into the picture that we were in a very bad position.<br />
&nbsp;</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2302, 115, 'Lesley''s husband David was diagnosed with a rare cancer and the doctors could not tell them much about what to expect, which Lesley found very difficult to cope with.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878672522');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2303, 115, 'Lesley''s husband David was diagnosed with a rare cancer and the doctors could not tell them much about what to expect, which Lesley found very difficult to cope with.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>We weren&rsquo;t offered any treatment options because nobody quite knew, until all the tests were done, what that was going to bring. So it was about a month and a half of puzzlement and, and everything was unclear and I&rsquo;m a very, I have to know person, so that I can deal. If I don&rsquo;t know I freak and I didn&rsquo;t know. So there was quite a bit of freaking on my part going on and, at the same time, trying to realise that I wasn&rsquo;t helping the situation, David or anything else.<br />
<br />
It was very difficult because as a disease itself, if it had been a solid tumour somewhere you can visualise excising it and dealing with it and, in apostrophes, curing it but to be told that what David had was treatable but not curable you you&rsquo;ve got this long distant future in front of you, which is grossly unknown, and I don&rsquo;t deal well with the unknown. I deal with knowing what I&rsquo;m doing, where I&rsquo;m going. I don&rsquo;t deal well if things are unknown and I found me a bit crumbly really because it wasn&rsquo;t obvious what was going to happen or how we were going to deal with what was going to happen, both medically and domestically, how that was going to pan out either. And, particularly, with the state we found ourselves in, in the house as well, which hadn&rsquo;t even been started let alone finished. All of that just fell into a big hole and I couldn&rsquo;t work out what was going to happen first or how it was going to happen and that was confusing in the extreme.<br />
<br />
I think that was probably, even more confounded by the fact that the medical experts, they didn&rsquo;t know either. So they couldn&rsquo;t give you any reassurance that if they did that, then that would happen and if they did something else, then that would happen, because they didn&rsquo;t know either. So that was even more, for me, that was even more confusing and I&rsquo;m not very patient. I&rsquo;m not a patient patient. David&rsquo;s very patient but I&rsquo;m not a patient patient. And I was getting impatient with them, which is why I spent such a long time on line trying to fathom my way through it myself. So that was immediately, for me, a huge problem because I couldn&rsquo;t, not being in control, I couldn&rsquo;t make it better. I couldn&rsquo;t get anybody else to say that they were going to make it better either and that was very hard, very, very hard indeed all round.<br />
&nbsp;</p></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<div>The diagnosis could be unexpected, so carers were not always present when the patient was given the news. Mary&rsquo;s husband was admitted to A&amp;E while she was away in France; she returned to find him in hospital. Julie&lsquo;s partner came home from a hospital appointment with his diagnosis of oesophageal cancer and said &lsquo;read this&rsquo;. It can be difficult to come to terms with a terminal prognosis and carers were often left feeling numb and were faced with telling members of their family and friends.</div>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2304, 115, 'After learning his wife''s diagnosis Simon felt he was burdened with carrying a bomb because he knew what a shock it would be for the family.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878673091');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2305, 115, 'After learning his wife''s diagnosis Simon felt he was burdened with carrying a bomb because he knew what a shock it would be for the family.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>It felt to me like carrying a bomb. It felt to me like carrying a bomb that if I opened my mouth would pull the pin, you know. And it was, you know as though we were, as though I was sort of cradling this news.<br />
<br />
<strong>Who did you feel you wanted to tell first? Who?</strong><br />
<br />
Oh well I didn&rsquo;t want to tell anyone. I mean I, it was a case of we were going to have to tell, my parents were here, they&rsquo;d come to look after the children. While we went to the appointment. Or I think it was just my mum actually and, so we knew that we had to tell them. And Karen&rsquo;s family. But it was a case of have, we had to, you know. We, we didn&rsquo;t want to tell anyone. Because we knew how, we knew what a shock it was for us. Or this is how I felt at least that it was such a shock that I didn&rsquo;t want to burden anyone with that.<br />
&nbsp;</p></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<div>Deciding what to do once a terminal diagnosis has been given can be difficult. Some carers decided not to tell the patient the prognosis. Henry and Jane&rsquo;s children decided it was important that Jane felt she could recover, and Saba&rsquo;s family decided if her mum didn&rsquo;t ask they wouldn&rsquo;t tell her about the extent of her cancer.<br />
&nbsp;</div>

<div>When the patient knew of the prognosis the decisions about what to do next were usually shared and the time left was highly valued. Dick and Di went on a holiday they had always dreamed of to Venice, and Anne planned her 50<sup>th</sup> wedding anniversary party - she felt &lsquo;if we don&rsquo;t do it now, we never will&rsquo;. Victoria and Ivan felt they would take one day at a time and used their faith to keep them strong.<br />
&nbsp;</div>

<div>Being told that the condition was terminal meant that certain treatments (such as a Whipple&rsquo;s operation for pancreatic cancer) were no longer an option or that there was no treatment available other than palliative care. Palliative care aims to provide relief for symptoms and emotional and spiritual support to patients and carers to enable the patient to live life as fully as possible. Once the prognosis was understood some patients rejected any further treatments aimed at prolonging life, such as chemotherapy or radiotherapy, in favour of symptom control.</div>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2306, 115, 'Although some of the health professionals advocated radiotherapy to try to prolong Donald''s life, he and Maggie chose palliative care only.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878672490');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2307, 115, 'Although some of the health professionals advocated radiotherapy to try to prolong Donald''s life, he and Maggie chose palliative care only.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>I went in one day and there was a nurse. She was a she was a proper, what I would call a Sloane Ranger, beautifully spoken, and she came bustling up to me and said, she&rsquo;d been talking to Donald and she&rsquo;d been trying to get him to look on the bright side and she said&hellip; I think he we must have had the results of the biopsy by then, pretty much, but we were waiting to speak to the neurologist about it because, she said, &ldquo;He should think positively. He should, he might be able to go home. It wasn&rsquo;t a bad diagnosis.&rdquo; And I looked at her and she said, &ldquo;Well, actually.&rdquo; She said, &ldquo;It&rsquo;s not a wonderful diagnosis.&rdquo; She said, &ldquo;But, you know, with a bit of positive thinking he might be able to go home and get some radiotherapy and it&rsquo;d give him a bit longer.&rdquo; And I just looked at her absolutely amazed because that wasn&rsquo;t what I&rsquo;d been told.<br />
<br />
So then I was talking to Donald and sort of trying to look on the bright side but he wasn&rsquo;t having any of it and, eventually, we saw the neurologist and he said he&rsquo;d got the histology back and it was a high grade astrocytoma. When I said, how it had it been missed the first time, he said it presented backwards. Normally, apparently, the core comes first and then the shading, the nucleus round it but Donald had present differently and also, astrocytomas are more prevalent in children. So that was why they&rsquo;d missed it.<br />
<br />
So then we had this case conference, and this conversation about, you know, whether he should be allowed to die or whether it had, and I said, &ldquo;Look. Donald&rsquo;s had enough.&rdquo; I said, &ldquo;And it&rsquo;s not because he&rsquo;s depressed and it&rsquo;s not because he&rsquo;s cross and he&rsquo;s not because he&rsquo;s given up. I said we&rsquo;ve always said, right from the very beginning both of us that, if it&rsquo;s inevitable then let&rsquo;s get it over with.&rdquo; So the neurologist then acquiesced that Donald would be left alone and to get palliative care in and he could have what he wanted, which is a quiet and reasonably dignified death.<br />
&nbsp;</p></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<div>The term &lsquo;palliative care&rsquo; is not always fully understood by people and the realisation that there are no further potentially curative treatments can be upsetting. The possibility of taking part in clinical trials was investigated by several, as were complementary therapies for symptoms. Although they had been told the condition was terminal, some carers still hoped for a cure or said that the seriousness of the situation only dawned on them slowly over time.</div>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2308, 115, 'Dick and Di investigated, but decided against, using complementary therapies; they continued to hope for a cure despite knowing Di''s MND was terminal.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878672967');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2309, 115, 'Dick and Di investigated, but decided against, using complementary therapies; they continued to hope for a cure despite knowing Di''s MND was terminal.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>It was a really strange sort of six months, you know, because, you know, we were convinced and at the same time we were hoping, you know, and, and it was, we were planning what, what on earth were we going to do and how long might it take you know and the&hellip; what we&rsquo;d read suggesting anything between eighteen months and ten years possibly. So what was going to happen, how would we deal with it?<br />
<br />
And I got in touch with a physician who I knew that I used to work with because I used to work in a complementary health clinic, who specialised in life threatening diseases. And he came back to us and made some suggestions.<br />
<br />
But I think it was very clear&hellip; both to Di and to myself that we didn&rsquo;t really want to go down that route. We didn&rsquo;t really want to anxiously fight the whole thing you know. You know, MND the outcome was pretty certain whatever you did. And, I mean, I guess we both held out for the possibility of some unusual cure or, or prolongation of the disease. But we both realised that, you know, that it was terminal.<br />
&nbsp;</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2310, 115, 'Initially Peter thought his friend would get better.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878672556');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2311, 115, 'Initially Peter thought his friend would get better.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p><strong>When we look, you look back at [friend&rsquo;s name]&rsquo;s illness, initially did you expect, what did you expect to happen after her initial diagnosis?</strong><br />
<br />
I expected her to have the chemo, as people do, and improve and progress.<br />
<br />
I was going to move back to my place, fifteen mile away and see her at weekends. And she was she was going to go back to work as a receptionist, a wages clerk and continue her old life and I&rsquo;d have my life and meet up at weekends, be friends and partners. <br />
<br />
<strong>So did you view caring just as an interlude where you&rsquo;d be helping her over a difficult time?<br />
</strong><br />
Basically, yes. Initially, yes, I suppose. Just wanted her to get better and I can get her better.<br />
And, I was very optimistic and confident.<br />
&nbsp;</p></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<div>Although many carers still hoped for an improvement, honesty from the medical professionals was highly valued. For Georgina, honesty and openness from the consultants treating her mother&rsquo;s cancer helped her to feel involved in the treatment decisions. Jane found it frustrating when some of the professionals dealing with George&rsquo;s care were not totally honest with her. It can be difficult for the consultant to gauge accurately how long a patient has to live, although many wanted to know. Some carers said it was easier to cope if they knew what the life expectancy would be; one said that knowing it could be several years was helpful. Heather asked the consultant outright, &lsquo;How long have we got?&rsquo; Sarah realised she was &lsquo;in for the long haul&rsquo; when she started caring for her parents who both had Parkinson&rsquo;s disease. Sometimes the time between diagnosis and death was very short, or shorter than expected, and carers found they had little time to adjust. Others said that it had been difficult to adjust when the ill person had lived for longer than expected due to the availability of new treatments.</div>

<p>
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2312, 115, 'When things started to go wrong Cassie asked the doctor if her dad was going to die and was grateful for his honesty; she then asked her father if he thought he would die.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878672696');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2313, 115, 'When things started to go wrong Cassie asked the doctor if her dad was going to die and was grateful for his honesty; she then asked her father if he thought he would die.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>And then he had hundreds of things that went wrong. His lungs kept collapsing. I was, at one point, he was in hospital and he was so ill that I thought he was going to die that day. I took him to hospital and he was being sick constantly. He looked like he was dying and I said to the doctor at the time, &ldquo;He&rsquo;s going to die isn&rsquo;t he?&rdquo; And the doctor said, &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know.&rdquo; And it was just the realist thing anybody had said to me because we&rsquo;d always, we&rsquo;d constantly been told, &ldquo;Hold on, hold out hope. You can survive it. You can survive it.&rdquo; And this doctor said, &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know.&rdquo; And, although it wasn&rsquo;t conclusive, it wasn&rsquo;t giving me an answer, it was real and so, at that point, I spoke to my dad and said, &ldquo;Do you think you&rsquo;re going to die?&rdquo; And he said, &ldquo;Yeah, I won&rsquo;t get through this.&rdquo;<br />
<br />
<br />
And that was such a difficult question to ask and I was really nervous about asking it and a bit sort of embarrassed and, oh, you know, and when he said, &ldquo;Yes.&rdquo; I thought, &ldquo;Oh, right well, if he thinks he&rsquo;s going to die then, you know, that&rsquo;s really it.&rdquo;<br />
&nbsp;</p></section></div>
</div>
  
<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2314, 115, 'Over time Jane came to believe that knowing George''s MND was incurable was easier to deal with than if it had been cancer where new treatments often become available.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878673129');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2315, 115, 'Over time Jane came to believe that knowing George''s MND was incurable was easier to deal with than if it had been cancer where new treatments often become available.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p>Interestingly, as we kind of went through the journey I actually, came to the conclusion that in some ways, that was better than having cancer because we knew absolutely what we were facing. We knew that there was no option. That actually, you know, we were facing certain death with no treatment at all. Whereas if he&rsquo;d had cancer, we&rsquo;d have been striving constantly for the next treatment and the next treatment and the next treatment no matter how narrow the chances were of a cure, we would still have been striving for, for treatment but, actually, the thing that it gave us was this incredible honesty between us in our communication because we had to be honest because it was, you know, we had to say the things that needed to be said because we knew that there would come a time when we couldn&rsquo;t say them.&nbsp;</p></section></div>
</div>
  </p>

<p>Last reviewed December 2017.</p></div><p>Copyright &copy; 2024 University of Oxford. All rights reserved.</p>', NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO articles (id, subcategory_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (116, 116, 'How bad news was broken', '<h1>Living with dying</h1>
      <h2>How bad news was broken</h2><div class=''content''><p><span class="BodyText">In other&nbsp;Health Experience Insightscollections people explain how they were told about their diagnosis of cancer, heart failure or other serious illness. Here we discuss how they were told that life would be shorter than expected.</span><br />
<br />
<span class="BodyText">It is not easy to break any bad news and it is particularly hard to tell patients that their disease has come back or that there are no more treatments that might lead to a cure.</span><br />
<br />
<span class="BodyText">Most patients want their doctors to be truthful, caring and compassionate, to speak in simple terms, and to use clear cut language, but doctors still have to decide when and how to tell the patient that they are unlikely to have long to live. Some patients decided to be open about their suspicion that the disease was terminal. A man with testicular cancer describes how he seemed to help his doctors by being open.</span></p>

<div class=''vbox tVideo v4x3 ''>
  <div class=''ttitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878633771');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2316, 116, 'A man was open about his poor prognosis and that seemed to help his doctors.', '</div>
  <div class=''tb''>
    <div class=''vboxi''></div>
    <div class=''rcol''>
      <div class=''rtitle''>', NULL, 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/878633771');
INSERT INTO sub_articles (id, article_id, title, content, image_url, video_url) VALUES (2317, 116, 'A man was open about his poor prognosis and that seemed to help his doctors.', '</div>
      
    </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class=''vbot'' >
    <div class=''showHide''><i class=''fa fas fa-chevron-right''></i>SHOW TEXT<span class=''long''> VERSION</span></div>
    
    <div class=''printBlock''><i class=''fa fas fa-print''></i>PRINT<span class=''long''> TRANSCRIPT</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class=''transcript''><section><p><span class="Interviewee">Now when my doctor turned round to me and said, "Well there''s this illness called focal segmental&nbsp;</span>bromular sclerosis," I was trying to work out whether he continued the discussion in English or it was a foreign language. He was fumbling with his notes and embarrassed and I said, "It''s terminal isn''t it?" so he said, "Yes it is," and there was that instance. I''m taken into hospital a lot because my bladder goes into spasm or I get uncontrollable pain that needs intravenous morphine which I can''t do myself, and when the doctors sort of look through the notes and they come in and they say, the first thing I say to them is, "Look I know it''s terminal so you don''t have to sort of beat about the bush," and the look of relief on their faces when I''ve told them, it''s as though phew, thank God we don''t have to go through that. People don''t like giving bad news and I believe that they, there is no nice way of saying it, you don’t walk in and say, "You''re going to die," no one does that not unless they''ve got the most incredibly bad bedside manner. But you tell, somebody would want to know, I know I would want to know.<!--[if gte mso 9]><xml>
